/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/aws-developer.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("aws-developer", [["dva1",0,1,"A Lambda function consumes an SQS queue in batches of 10. When one message fails, all 10 are returned to the queue and reprocessed. How can the developer retry only the failed messages?",["Raise the queue's visibility timeout to 12 hours so retries happen less often","Convert the standard queue to a FIFO queue with content-based deduplication","Set the function's reserved concurrency to 1 so batches run one at a time","Enable ReportBatchItemFailures and return the failed message IDs"],3,"With ReportBatchItemFailures the function returns batchItemFailures, so Lambda deletes the successes and only the listed messages become visible again. A longer visibility timeout just delays the same full-batch retry.","Task 1.2",["A longer visibility timeout only delays the retry; the whole batch of 10 still becomes visible again when one message fails.","FIFO with content-based deduplication prevents duplicate sends, but a failed batch is still retried as a whole without partial batch responses.","Reserved concurrency of 1 only serializes batches; each failing batch is still returned to the queue in full.",null],3],["dva2",0,1,"An orders table must return all orders for one customer, newest first, using a single efficient request. Which key design fits?",["Partition key orderDate and sort key customerId","Partition key customerId and sort key orderDate","Partition key orderId with no sort key","Partition key status and sort key customerId"],1,"A Query on partition key customerId returns that customer's items, and ScanIndexForward=false sorts them by orderDate descending. Using orderId alone would force a Scan.","Task 1.3",["With orderDate as the partition key, one customer's orders are scattered across many partition values and cannot be fetched with a single Query.",null,"With only orderId as the key, finding one customer's orders requires a Scan, and there is no sort key to order by date.","A status partition key groups orders by status, not by customer, and a low-cardinality key like status also creates hot partitions."],1],["dva3",0,1,"A DynamoDB table already exists and holds data. The team now needs to query items by an email attribute that is not part of the primary key. What should they add?",["A local secondary index with email as its sort key","A DynamoDB Streams trigger on the table","A global secondary index with email as its partition key","A second attribute named email in the table's primary key"],2,"GSIs can be added to an existing table and can use any attribute as a new partition key. LSIs can only be defined when the table is created and must share the table's partition key.","Task 1.3",["An LSI can only be created together with the table and must reuse the table's partition key, so it cannot be added now or be keyed on email.","Streams capture item changes for processing; they do not give you a way to query items by email.",null,"A table's primary key cannot be changed after creation; you would have to create a new table and migrate the data."],2],["dva4",0,1,"Which DynamoDB read operation cannot use strongly consistent reads?",["A Query against a global secondary index","A GetItem on the base table","A Query against a local secondary index","A Scan of the base table"],0,"GSIs are updated asynchronously and support only eventually consistent reads. Base-table reads and LSI queries can request ConsistentRead=true.","Task 1.3",[null,"GetItem on the base table supports ConsistentRead=true, so strongly consistent reads are available there.","LSIs share the base table's partition and support strongly consistent reads when ConsistentRead=true is set.","A Scan of the base table can also request ConsistentRead=true, so it does support strong consistency."],2],["dva5",0,1,"An application performs 10 strongly consistent reads per second of items that are 6 KB each. How many read capacity units does the table need?",["20","10","15","60"],0,"One RCU covers one strongly consistent read per second of up to 4 KB. 6 KB rounds up to 8 KB, which is 2 RCUs per read, so 10 reads need 20. Eventually consistent reads would need 10.","Task 1.3",[null,"10 RCU would cover 6 KB items only with eventually consistent reads; strongly consistent reads of 6 KB need 2 RCU each.","15 comes from dividing 6 KB by 4 KB without rounding up; capacity is billed in whole 4 KB units, so each read costs 2 RCU.","60 treats RCU as 1 KB units (the write unit size); read units are 4 KB, so 6 KB needs 2 RCU per read."],3],["dva6",0,1,"An application writes 12 items per second to DynamoDB, and each item is 2.5 KB. How many write capacity units are required?",["30","36","12","24"],1,"One WCU covers one write per second of up to 1 KB. 2.5 KB rounds up to 3 KB, so 3 WCUs per write, times 12 writes, gives 36.","Task 1.3",["30 uses 2.5 KB without rounding up; each write is billed in whole 1 KB units, so 2.5 KB costs 3 WCU.",null,"12 would only be enough for items of 1 KB or less; a 2.5 KB item needs 3 WCU per write.","24 rounds 2.5 KB down to 2 KB; DynamoDB always rounds item size up, to 3 KB here."],3],["dva7",0,1,"Two users edit the same DynamoDB item at the same time, and one user's change silently overwrites the other's. How should the developer prevent lost updates?",["Switch every read in the application to eventually consistent mode","Add a global secondary index on a version attribute and query it first","Turn on DynamoDB Streams and replay the lost changes from the stream","Store a version number and add a condition expression that checks it on write"],3,"Optimistic locking compares the expected version in a ConditionExpression; if another writer changed it first, the write fails with ConditionalCheckFailedException instead of overwriting.","Task 1.3",["Read consistency affects how fresh a read is, not whether a later write overwrites a concurrent one; eventual reads make the problem worse.","A GSI on a version attribute is eventually consistent and a separate read, so a race between read and write remains.","Streams record changes after they happen; replaying them does not stop the overwrite and cannot decide which edit should win.",null],3],["dva8",0,1,"A Lambda function opens a new database connection and loads a large config file on every invocation, adding latency. What change helps most?",["Increase the function timeout to 15 minutes so the setup has time to finish","Copy the config file into the function's /tmp folder at the start of every call","Create the connection and load the config outside the handler for reuse","Invoke the function asynchronously so callers do not wait for the setup work"],2,"Code outside the handler runs once per execution environment and is reused by warm invocations. A longer timeout or async invocation does not remove the repeated setup work.","Task 1.2",["A longer timeout lets the slow setup finish but still repeats it on every invocation, so latency stays the same.","Copying the file to /tmp on every call is still repeated work; /tmp persists in warm environments, but only code outside the handler avoids redoing it.",null,"Async invocation hides latency from callers but the function still repeats the same setup on every run and costs the same duration."],2],["dva9",0,1,"A nightly batch job takes about 40 minutes to run in a single process. The team wants a serverless option with no servers to manage. Where should it run?",["A single Lambda function with the maximum timeout","An API Gateway REST API with a Lambda integration","A Lambda@Edge function on CloudFront","An Amazon ECS task on AWS Fargate"],3,"Lambda functions stop after at most 15 minutes, so a 40-minute single process does not fit. Fargate runs containers for as long as needed without managing EC2 instances.","Task 1.2",["Lambda's maximum timeout is 15 minutes, so a 40-minute single process would be stopped partway through.","API Gateway integrations time out after about 29 seconds by default, and Lambda behind it is still capped at 15 minutes.","Lambda@Edge has even tighter limits (seconds, not minutes) and is meant for request/response processing at CloudFront, not batch jobs.",null],3],["dva10",0,1,"An S3 event invokes a Lambda function asynchronously. Events that still fail after Lambda's built-in retries are being lost. What should the developer configure?",["Configure an on-failure destination or a dead-letter queue","Increase the function's memory so the retries are more likely to succeed","Configure provisioned concurrency so every event gets a warm environment","Lower the maximum event age to 60 seconds so failures are reported sooner"],0,"For asynchronous invocations, Lambda retries twice by default and then discards the event unless an on-failure destination or DLQ is set. A shorter event age would drop events even sooner.","Task 1.2",[null,"More memory may help some retries succeed, but events that still fail after the retries are still discarded without a destination or DLQ.","Provisioned concurrency reduces cold starts; it does nothing to capture events that fail processing.","Lowering the maximum event age makes Lambda discard events sooner; without a destination or DLQ they are still lost."],2],["dva11",0,1,"A Lambda function attached to private subnets in a VPC must call a public third-party API on the internet. What is required?",["A public IP address assigned to the function's elastic network interfaces","An internet gateway route added directly to the private subnets' route table","A NAT gateway in a public subnet, routed from the private subnets","A VPC gateway endpoint that points to the third-party API's domain name"],2,"Lambda ENIs in a VPC never get public IPs, so outbound internet access goes through a NAT gateway. Gateway endpoints exist only for S3 and DynamoDB. Note that NAT gateways are billed hourly.","Task 1.2",["Lambda's elastic network interfaces in a VPC never receive public IP addresses, and you cannot assign one.","A route to an internet gateway makes the subnets public, but the function's ENIs still have no public IP, so traffic cannot get out.",null,"Gateway endpoints exist only for S3 and DynamoDB; they cannot reach arbitrary third-party internet APIs."],3],["dva12",0,1,"Twelve Lambda functions use the same in-house logging library. How can the team share it without bundling a copy into each deployment package?",["Store the library's code in an environment variable read by each function","Publish the library as a Lambda layer and attach it to each function","Download the library into each function's /tmp folder on every cold start","Run the library as a separate state in an AWS Step Functions state machine"],1,"Layers package shared libraries or runtimes once and attach them to many functions. Environment variables hold small strings, not code.","Task 1.2",["Environment variables are limited to 4 KB in total and are meant for configuration strings, not library code.",null,"Downloading on each cold start adds latency and an external dependency, and it still keeps a copy per function; layers solve this directly.","Step Functions orchestrates separate tasks; a logging library must run inside each function's process, not as a workflow state."],1],["dva13",0,1,"A developer wants a workflow that charges a card, waits (possibly for days) for a human to approve a refund, then continues, with retries on each step. Which service fits best?",["A single Lambda function that polls in a loop","An SQS standard queue with a long visibility timeout","Amazon EventBridge with a scheduled rule","AWS Step Functions with a task token callback"],3,"Step Functions Standard workflows can pause on a .waitForTaskToken task until SendTaskSuccess is called, and they provide per-state retry and catch. A polling Lambda would hit the 15-minute limit.","Task 1.1",["A single Lambda function stops after 15 minutes, so it cannot wait days, and polling in a loop wastes compute.","SQS visibility timeout tops out at 12 hours and a queue has no workflow logic, per-step retries or approval callbacks.","A scheduled EventBridge rule runs on a timer; it does not track workflow state or pause until a human responds.",null],2],["dva14",0,1,"When an order is placed, three independent services (billing, shipping, analytics) must each receive and process every order event. Which design is most appropriate?",["Send each order to one SQS queue that all three services poll","Write each order to an S3 bucket and have every service list the bucket","Publish to an SNS topic with one SQS queue subscribed per service","Invoke the three services one after another from the producer"],2,"SNS-to-SQS fan-out delivers a copy of each message to every subscribed queue, so each service processes all events at its own pace. With one shared queue, each message would go to only one consumer.","Task 1.1",["With one shared queue each message is delivered to only one of the competing consumers, so each service would miss most orders.","Listing a bucket is slow, has no delivery tracking and forces each service to work out which objects it has already processed.",null,"Invoking services in sequence couples the producer to all three and a failure or slowdown in one blocks or breaks the rest."],2],["dva15",0,1,"An SQS FIFO queue carries updates for many customers. Updates must stay in order for each customer, but different customers should be processed in parallel. What should the producer set?",["The MessageDeduplicationId to the customer ID","The MessageGroupId to the customer ID","The DelaySeconds to a different value for each customer","A separate FIFO queue for every message"],1,"FIFO ordering is kept within a message group, and different groups can be consumed in parallel. The deduplication ID only prevents duplicates within the 5-minute window.","Task 1.1",["The deduplication ID suppresses repeated messages with the same ID within 5 minutes, so using the customer ID would drop that customer's later updates.",null,"DelaySeconds only postpones delivery; FIFO queues support only a queue-level delay, and it has no effect on per-customer ordering or parallelism.","A queue per message is unmanageable and loses ordering across a customer's messages; message groups do this within one queue."],3],["dva16",0,1,"A consumer polls an SQS queue in a tight loop and receives many empty responses, increasing cost. What should the developer change?",["Enable long polling with a receive wait time of up to 20 seconds","Lower the queue's message retention period to reduce the number of messages","Shorten the visibility timeout to 0 seconds so messages come back faster","Convert the queue to a FIFO queue so messages are delivered in batches"],0,"Long polling waits for messages to arrive before responding, cutting empty receives and request charges. Visibility timeout and retention do not affect empty polls.","Task 1.1",[null,"Retention controls how long unconsumed messages are kept; it does not reduce empty responses from polling an empty queue.","A visibility timeout of 0 makes in-flight messages reappear immediately and causes duplicates; it does not reduce empty receives.","FIFO queues change ordering and deduplication; they still return empty responses under short polling."],1],["dva17",0,1,"Messages in an SQS queue are sometimes processed twice. Processing a message takes about 90 seconds, and the queue's visibility timeout is 30 seconds. What is the most likely fix?",["Enable long polling on the queue so consumers wait for new messages","Increase the message retention period to the maximum of 14 days","Raise the visibility timeout above the typical processing time","Add a 60-second delivery delay to each message the producer sends"],2,"If processing outlasts the visibility timeout, the message becomes visible again and another consumer picks it up. Setting the timeout longer than processing time (or extending it with ChangeMessageVisibility) prevents that.","Task 1.1",["Long polling reduces empty receives; it does not stop a message from reappearing while it is still being processed.","Retention decides how long unprocessed messages are kept, which is unrelated to duplicates caused by a short visibility timeout.",null,"A delivery delay only affects when a new message first becomes visible; after it is received, the 30-second visibility timeout still expires mid-processing."],3],["dva18",0,1,"A cleanup Lambda function must run every night at 02:00 UTC. What is the simplest way to trigger it?",["An Amazon EventBridge schedule with a cron expression","An SQS delay queue with a 24-hour delay","A CloudWatch alarm on the function's Invocations metric","An S3 lifecycle rule that invokes Lambda"],0,"EventBridge Scheduler (or a scheduled rule) invokes targets on cron or rate expressions. SQS delays are capped at 15 minutes.","Task 1.1",[null,"SQS delay queues cap at 15 minutes, so a 24-hour delay is not possible, and it would not run on a fixed clock time.","A CloudWatch alarm reacts to metric thresholds; it is not a scheduler and cannot invoke Lambda directly at a set time.","S3 lifecycle rules transition or expire objects; they cannot invoke Lambda or run on a schedule."],1],["dva19",0,1,"An application's AWS SDK calls occasionally fail with ThrottlingException during traffic spikes. What should the code do?",["Retry immediately in a tight loop until it succeeds","Retry with exponential backoff and jitter","Catch the error and discard the request","Create a new SDK client for each retry"],1,"AWS recommends exponential backoff with jitter so retries spread out and give the service time to recover. Immediate retries add load and make throttling worse.","Task 1.1",["Immediate retries in a tight loop add even more load while the service is already throttling, so failures continue.",null,"Discarding requests loses data or work; throttling is a transient error that should be retried with backoff.","A new client does not change the service's rate limit and wastes time on setup; the fix is spacing out retries."],1],["dva20",0,1,"A clickstream must be processed in order per user, kept for several days so it can be replayed, and read by multiple independent applications. Which service fits?",["Amazon SQS standard queue","Amazon SNS standard topic","Amazon SES","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams"],3,"Kinesis Data Streams keeps records ordered per shard (by partition key), retains them for replay and lets several consumers read the same data. SQS messages are removed once consumed.","Task 1.1",["SQS standard queues do not guarantee order, and a consumed message is deleted, so it cannot be replayed or shared by several apps.","SNS pushes messages to subscribers but does not store them for replay, and standard topics do not guarantee order.","Amazon SES is an email-sending service and has nothing to do with streaming clickstream data.",null],1],["dva21",0,1,"Which caching strategy updates the cache every time the database is written, so reads are never stale, at the cost of caching data that may never be read?",["Write-through","Lazy loading","Time-to-live only","Read replica"],0,"Write-through writes to the cache along with the database. Lazy loading fills the cache only on a miss, so it caches only requested data but can serve stale values.","Task 1.3",[null,"Lazy loading fills the cache only on a read miss, so it caches only requested data and can serve stale values after writes.","A TTL only expires entries after a set time; it does not update the cache when the database is written.","A read replica is a database copy for scaling reads; it is not a caching strategy and can lag behind the primary."],1],["dva22",0,1,"A DynamoDB table stores web sessions that should disappear 24 hours after creation without any cleanup code. What should the developer use?",["A nightly Scan that deletes old items with BatchWriteItem calls","A global secondary index on the session creation date attribute","DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) with a 24-hour item cache lifetime","DynamoDB TTL on an attribute that holds the expiry timestamp"],3,"TTL deletes items after the epoch time in the chosen attribute passes, at no extra write cost. A Scan-and-delete job works but consumes capacity and needs code.","Task 1.3",["A nightly Scan-and-delete job works but needs code, consumes read and write capacity, and removes items up to a day late.","A GSI only lets you query by creation date; it does not delete anything, so cleanup code would still be needed.","DAX caches reads in front of the table; a cache lifetime does not delete items from DynamoDB itself.",null],1],["dva23",0,1,"Whenever an item in a DynamoDB table changes, a Lambda function must send a notification that includes the old and new values. What should the developer configure?",["A CloudTrail trail that records DynamoDB data events for the table","DynamoDB Streams (new and old images) with a Lambda trigger","A scheduled Lambda function that scans the whole table every minute","An SNS topic subscribed directly to the table's change notifications"],1,"Streams record each item change, and the NEW_AND_OLD_IMAGES view type includes both versions; Lambda polls the stream through an event source mapping.","Task 1.3",["CloudTrail data events record who called which API, not the item's old and new attribute values, and they are not a push trigger for Lambda.",null,"A per-minute full Scan is expensive, misses intermediate changes and never sees the item's previous values.","DynamoDB cannot publish change notifications directly to an SNS topic; item-level changes are exposed through Streams."],2],["dva24",0,1,"An application uploads 3 GB video files to S3 and occasionally has to restart the whole upload after a network blip. What should it use?",["A single PutObject call with a longer timeout","S3 Transfer Acceleration without changing the upload method","Multipart upload, retrying only the failed parts","S3 Object Lock in governance mode"],2,"Multipart upload sends parts in parallel and lets a failed part be retried alone; AWS recommends it above about 100 MB and it is required above 5 GB. Transfer Acceleration can speed the path but still restarts a single PUT.","Task 1.1",["A single PutObject is limited to 5 GB and a network blip still forces the whole 3 GB upload to restart, whatever the timeout.","Transfer Acceleration can speed up the network path, but a single PUT that fails still has to start over from zero.",null,"Object Lock prevents objects from being deleted or overwritten; it does nothing to make uploads resumable."],2],["dva25",0,1,"A report runs a Scan with a FilterExpression on customerId, which is the table's partition key, and it is slow and expensive. What is the better approach?",["Increase the page size of the Scan","Use a parallel Scan with more segments","Use a Query with a key condition on customerId","Add a FilterExpression on another attribute as well"],2,"A Scan reads every item and filters afterward, consuming capacity for the whole table. A Query on the partition key reads only the matching items.","Task 1.3",["A larger page size only changes how many items come back per call; the Scan still reads and bills the whole table.","A parallel Scan finishes sooner but still reads every item and uses even more capacity at once.",null,"Filters are applied after the items are read, so another FilterExpression does not reduce the capacity consumed."],2],["dva26",0,1,"A read-heavy DynamoDB application needs microsecond read latency for repeated reads of the same items, without changing its data-access code much. What should the developer add?",["DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","A DynamoDB global table","An S3 bucket in front of the table","DynamoDB Streams"],0,"DAX is an API-compatible in-memory cache for DynamoDB that serves repeat reads in microseconds. Global tables replicate across Regions for availability, not caching.","Task 1.3",[null,"Global tables replicate data across Regions for availability and local writes; they do not add an in-memory cache.","S3 is object storage with millisecond latency and would require rewriting the data-access code; it is not a DynamoDB cache.","Streams deliver a log of item changes for processing; they do not speed up reads."],1],["dva27",0,1,"An API Gateway REST API with Lambda proxy integration returns 502 Bad Gateway. The function returns the plain string \"OK\". What is wrong?",["Enable CORS on the method so browsers accept the plain response","Return an object with statusCode, headers and a string body","Raise the function timeout above the 29-second integration limit","Add a mapping template that wraps the string in a JSON object"],1,"With proxy integration, API Gateway expects a JSON object containing statusCode and body (body as a string). Any other shape is a malformed Lambda response, which becomes a 502.","Task 1.1",["CORS only controls which browser origins may read a response; it does not fix the malformed proxy response that API Gateway turns into a 502.",null,"A timeout would produce a 504, not a 502, and the function already returns quickly; the problem is the shape of what it returns.","Proxy integrations pass the request and response through without mapping templates, so API Gateway still requires statusCode and body from the function."],3],["dva28",0,1,"An SQS standard queue can deliver the same message more than once. How should the consumer that charges customers be designed?",["Make it idempotent by storing each request ID and skipping repeats","Delete each message as soon as it is received, before charging the card","Set the visibility timeout to 0 seconds so duplicates show up at once","Switch the consumer's database reads to strongly consistent mode"],0,"Standard queues guarantee at-least-once delivery, so consumers must tolerate duplicates, for example with a conditional write on a request ID. Deleting first risks losing the message if processing fails.","Task 1.1",[null,"Deleting before processing means a crash mid-charge loses the message entirely, and it still does not stop a duplicate delivery from being charged.","A zero visibility timeout makes messages reappear immediately while still being processed, which causes more duplicate charges, not fewer.","Read consistency affects how fresh database reads are; it does not stop the same SQS message from being delivered and charged twice."],2],["dva29",0,2,"A Lambda function needs to read items from one DynamoDB table. How should it get permissions?",["Store an IAM user's access keys in the function's environment variables","Add a table policy that lets any principal in the account read the table","An execution role whose policy allows read actions on that one table","Attach the AdministratorAccess managed policy to the function's role"],2,"Lambda assumes its execution role and gets temporary credentials automatically; scoping the policy to the table follows least privilege. Long-lived IAM user keys are a risk and unnecessary.","Task 2.1",["Long-lived IAM user keys can leak and must be rotated by hand; Lambda already gets temporary credentials from its execution role.","Opening the table to every principal in the account breaks least privilege, and the function still needs an identity to make the calls.",null,"AdministratorAccess would work but grants far more than reading one table, violating least privilege."],1],["dva30",0,2,"An application on an EC2 instance must upload files to an S3 bucket. What is the recommended way to give it credentials?",["Save an IAM user's access keys in ~/.aws/credentials on the instance","Attach an IAM role to the instance through an instance profile","Hardcode the access keys in the application's source code","Make the bucket public so no credentials are needed"],1,"An instance profile delivers temporary, automatically rotated role credentials through the instance metadata service, and the SDK finds them without code changes.","Task 2.1",["Access keys stored on the instance are long-lived secrets that can leak and must be rotated manually; an instance role avoids them.",null,"Hardcoded keys end up in source control and builds, and are the most common way credentials leak.","Making the bucket public exposes its data to anyone, and uploads would need public write access, which violates least privilege."],1],["dva31",0,2,"A mobile app's users sign in with a Cognito user pool. The app then needs temporary AWS credentials to upload photos directly to S3. What provides those credentials?",["The user pool access token, sent to S3 in place of a SigV4 signature","A separate IAM user with its own access keys for every mobile user","A Cognito identity pool that trades the user pool token for role credentials","An API Gateway API key that the app includes in its S3 upload requests"],2,"User pools authenticate users and issue JWTs; identity pools federate those identities into temporary AWS credentials for an IAM role. S3 does not accept user pool tokens directly.","Task 2.1",["S3 only accepts SigV4-signed requests with AWS credentials; a user pool access token is a JWT that S3 does not understand.","An IAM user per mobile user does not scale (IAM has user quotas) and puts long-lived keys on devices.",null,"API keys identify clients for API Gateway usage plans; S3 does not use them and they are not AWS credentials."],3],["dva32",0,2,"A REST API in API Gateway must accept only requests from users signed in to an existing Cognito user pool, with the least custom code. What should the developer configure?",["A Cognito user pool authorizer on the API methods","A Lambda authorizer that validates the JWT","IAM authorization with SigV4 signing","An API key required on every method"],0,"A Cognito user pool authorizer validates the user pool token without custom code. A Lambda authorizer would also work but requires writing and maintaining validation logic.","Task 2.1",[null,"A Lambda authorizer could validate the JWT, but it requires writing and maintaining custom code, which the question wants to avoid.","IAM authorization needs AWS credentials and SigV4 signing; user pool sign-in alone does not give the client IAM credentials.","API keys only identify clients for usage plans; anyone with the key can call the API, so they do not prove a user signed in."],2],["dva33",0,2,"An API must validate bearer tokens issued by a third-party identity provider that uses a custom, non-standard token format. Which API Gateway feature fits?",["A Lambda authorizer","A Cognito user pool authorizer","A resource policy that limits source IPs","A usage plan with throttling"],0,"A Lambda authorizer runs your code to validate any token format and returns an IAM policy. Cognito authorizers only accept Cognito user pool tokens.","Task 2.1",[null,"A Cognito user pool authorizer accepts only tokens issued by a Cognito user pool, not a third party's custom format.","A resource policy can restrict source IPs, accounts or VPC endpoints, but it cannot validate a bearer token.","Usage plans throttle and meter clients identified by API keys; they do not validate tokens."],2],["dva34",0,2,"A backend service running with an IAM role must call an API Gateway API, and only that role should be allowed. Which authorization type should the API use?",["A Cognito user pool authorizer that checks the caller's ID token","An API key sent in the x-api-key header of every request","IAM authorization, with callers signing requests using SigV4","No authorization on the API, with an AWS WAF rule on the stage"],2,"IAM authorization checks the SigV4 signature against IAM policies, so access can be limited to a specific role. API keys identify clients for usage plans and are not an authorization mechanism.","Task 2.1",["Cognito authorizers validate user pool tokens for end users; a backend service with an IAM role has no user pool token.","API keys identify clients for usage plans; they can be shared or leaked and do not prove the caller is a specific IAM role.",null,"Leaving the API unauthenticated lets anyone call it; WAF filters traffic patterns but does not authenticate an IAM role."],2],["dva35",0,2,"An application in account A must write to a DynamoDB table in account B. What is the recommended approach?",["An IAM user in account B whose access keys are copied into account A","Account A's root user added to an administrators group in account B","A table resource policy in account B that allows any principal to write","A role in account B that trusts account A, assumed with sts:AssumeRole"],3,"Cross-account access uses a role whose trust policy names account A; the caller also needs sts:AssumeRole permission and receives temporary credentials. Sharing long-lived keys is not recommended.","Task 2.1",["Copying long-lived IAM user keys between accounts is a security risk and needs manual rotation; roles give temporary credentials.","The root user should never be used for applications, and root users cannot be added to IAM groups in another account.","A policy allowing any principal to write would open the table to everyone, not just account A, which breaks least privilege.",null],3],["dva36",0,2,"An application uses an Amazon RDS database password that must be rotated automatically every 30 days with minimal custom work. Where should the password be stored?",["Systems Manager Parameter Store as a String parameter","AWS Secrets Manager with rotation enabled","An encrypted environment variable on the Lambda function","A text file in a private S3 bucket"],1,"Secrets Manager has built-in rotation for RDS credentials. Parameter Store can hold SecureStrings but has no built-in rotation, so you would build it yourself.","Task 2.3",["A plain String parameter is not encrypted, and Parameter Store has no built-in rotation for RDS passwords.",null,"Lambda environment variables have no rotation feature; you would have to update and redeploy the value yourself.","A file in S3 has no rotation, and the application would have to fetch and parse it; it is not a secrets store."],1],["dva37",0,2,"A team needs to store feature settings and a few encrypted API endpoints that never rotate, at the lowest cost. What should they use?",["AWS Secrets Manager, with a separate secret created for every single value","Parameter Store standard parameters, with SecureString for sensitive values","Amazon DynamoDB items encrypted on the client with the Encryption SDK","A separate AWS KMS customer managed key that stores each setting value"],1,"Standard Parameter Store parameters have no extra storage charge and SecureString encrypts with KMS. Secrets Manager bills per secret per month and mainly adds rotation, which is not needed here.","Task 2.3",["Secrets Manager charges per secret per month and mainly adds rotation, which these static values do not need.",null,"Encrypting items in DynamoDB with the Encryption SDK works but adds code and table costs that Parameter Store avoids.","KMS keys encrypt data; they do not store configuration values, and each customer managed key costs about $1 per month."],2],["dva38",0,2,"An application must encrypt 50 MB files with a KMS key. Calling the KMS Encrypt API fails because of the payload size. What should the developer do?",["Split the file into 4 KB chunks and call the KMS Encrypt API for each chunk","Ask AWS Support to raise the Encrypt payload limit for the account","Use GenerateDataKey and encrypt the file locally with the data key","Store the file unencrypted and use KMS to encrypt only the S3 object key"],2,"KMS Encrypt accepts at most 4 KB. Envelope encryption uses a data key to encrypt the data locally and keeps only the wrapped key, which KMS can decrypt later.","Task 2.2",["Chunking into 4 KB calls would work but is slow, expensive and throttle-prone; envelope encryption is the intended pattern.","The 4 KB Encrypt limit is a fixed service limit, not an adjustable quota that Support can raise.",null,"Encrypting only the object key name leaves the file contents in plaintext, which does not meet the requirement."],2],["dva39",0,2,"A compliance team wants every use of the key that encrypts S3 objects to appear in CloudTrail, and wants to control the key policy themselves. Which encryption option fits?",["SSE-KMS with a customer managed key","SSE-S3 with S3 managed keys","SSE-C with keys supplied on every request","No server-side encryption, relying on TLS"],0,"SSE-KMS logs KMS API calls in CloudTrail, and a customer managed key lets the team write the key policy. SSE-S3 keys are managed entirely by S3 and have no key policy you control.","Task 2.2",[null,"SSE-S3 keys are fully managed by S3; there is no key policy to control and no per-use KMS entries in CloudTrail.","With SSE-C, the customer supplies the key on each request and S3 does not store it; there is no KMS key policy or KMS key-usage log.","TLS protects data only in transit; objects would sit unencrypted at rest and there would be no key usage to audit."],2],["dva40",0,2,"An application writes thousands of objects per second to a bucket that uses SSE-KMS and starts receiving KMS throttling errors. What reduces KMS requests with the least change?",["Switch to client-side encryption with the Encryption SDK","Create a new KMS key for each object","Disable default encryption on the bucket","Enable S3 Bucket Keys for the bucket"],3,"An S3 Bucket Key creates a short-lived bucket-level key that S3 uses to derive data keys, cutting calls to KMS and their cost.","Task 2.2",["Client-side encryption with the Encryption SDK still calls KMS for data keys unless caching is added, and it requires significant code changes.","A key per object multiplies the number of keys and KMS calls, making throttling and cost worse.","Disabling default encryption does not remove the SSE-KMS requirement set by the application or policy, and would weaken protection.",null],3],["dva41",0,2,"A bucket policy must reject any request that is not sent over HTTPS. Which condition should a Deny statement use?",["s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption equal to AES256","aws:SourceIp not in the corporate range","aws:SecureTransport equal to false","s3:signatureversion equal to AWS"],2,"Denying requests where aws:SecureTransport is false enforces encryption in transit. The server-side encryption header controls encryption at rest, not transport.","Task 2.2",["The server-side encryption header governs encryption at rest; it says nothing about whether the request used HTTPS.","aws:SourceIp restricts where requests come from, not whether they are encrypted in transit.",null,"s3:signatureversion checks the signing version (SigV2 or SigV4), not whether the connection uses TLS."],1],["dva42",0,2,"A web app lets users upload a profile photo directly to a private S3 bucket without giving them AWS credentials. What should the backend generate?",["A public-read ACL on the bucket for the duration of the upload","An IAM user with access keys created for each end user","A CloudFront signed cookie that grants read access to the bucket","A presigned PutObject URL that expires after a few minutes"],3,"A presigned URL carries the signer's permission for one operation on one object until it expires, so the bucket stays private.","Task 2.2",["A public-read ACL exposes the bucket's objects to everyone and does not grant upload permission, so the bucket would no longer be private.","Creating IAM users and keys for end users hands out long-lived AWS credentials, which is exactly what the app must avoid.","Signed cookies grant read access to content through CloudFront; they do not let a user upload an object to the bucket.",null],2],["dva43",0,2,"A developer is attaching an ACM certificate to a CloudFront distribution for a custom domain, but the certificate does not appear in the list. What is the most likely reason?",["ACM certificates cannot be used with CloudFront","The certificate was requested in a Region other than US East (N. Virginia)","The certificate must be imported, not requested","CloudFront needs the certificate stored in Secrets Manager"],1,"CloudFront only uses ACM certificates from us-east-1. For a Regional API Gateway endpoint or ALB, the certificate must be in the same Region as the resource.","Task 2.2",["ACM certificates are the standard way to add a custom-domain TLS certificate to CloudFront; they just have to be in us-east-1.",null,"Both ACM-issued and imported certificates work with CloudFront; importing is not required.","CloudFront reads certificates from ACM (or IAM), not from Secrets Manager."],1],["dva44",0,2,"A developer's script works locally because it reads access keys hardcoded in the source file. The script will run on Lambda next. What is the best practice?",["Remove the keys and let the SDK use the function's execution role","Move the same access keys into the function's environment variables","Encrypt the hardcoded keys with KMS and decrypt them on each call","Store the keys in a config file packaged in the deployment .zip"],0,"The SDK's default provider chain finds role credentials automatically on Lambda, EC2 and ECS. Environment variable keys are still long-lived secrets that can leak.","Task 2.3",[null,"Environment variables still hold long-lived keys that can leak through the console, logs or the function configuration.","Encrypting the keys still leaves long-lived credentials to manage and rotate, when the execution role provides temporary ones for free.","A config file in the package is still a hardcoded long-lived secret that anyone with access to the package can read."],1],["dva45",0,2,"S3 must invoke a Lambda function when objects are created, but the event notification fails to save with a permissions error. What is missing?",["An s3:PutBucketNotification permission in the function's execution role","A resource-based policy on the function that lets S3 invoke it","A bucket policy that grants the function s3:GetObject on new objects","SSE-KMS encryption enabled as the source bucket's default encryption"],1,"Services that push events to Lambda need permission in the function's resource-based policy (lambda add-permission). The execution role controls what the function itself can do.","Task 2.1",["The execution role controls what the function can do, not who may invoke it; S3 needs permission in the function's resource-based policy.",null,"A bucket policy governs access to objects; the failure is that S3 is not allowed to invoke the function.","Bucket encryption settings have nothing to do with S3's permission to invoke a Lambda function."],3],["dva46",0,2,"A user has an IAM policy allowing s3:* on a bucket, and the bucket policy explicitly denies s3:DeleteObject for that user. What happens when the user tries to delete an object?",["The request is denied, because an explicit deny overrides any allow","The request succeeds, because the identity policy allows s3:*","The request succeeds only if the object is unencrypted","The result depends on which policy was created most recently"],0,"In IAM policy evaluation, an explicit deny anywhere wins over any allow. Order of creation is irrelevant.","Task 2.1",[null,"An allow never overrides an explicit deny; the s3:* allow is cancelled for DeleteObject by the bucket policy's deny.","Encryption status has no effect on this evaluation; the explicit deny applies to every DeleteObject call by that user.","Policy evaluation does not depend on creation order; all applicable policies are evaluated together and explicit deny wins."],2],["dva47",0,2,"After signing in to a Cognito user pool, which token contains claims about the user such as email and name for the app to display?",["The access token","The refresh token","The session token from STS","The ID token"],3,"The ID token carries identity claims about the user. The access token carries scopes and groups for authorizing API calls, and the refresh token gets new tokens.","Task 2.1",["The access token carries scopes and groups for authorizing API calls, not the profile claims meant for display.","The refresh token is opaque and is only used to obtain new ID and access tokens.","An STS session token belongs to temporary AWS credentials (for example from an identity pool) and holds no user profile claims.",null],1],["dva48",0,2,"A Lambda function in account A must decrypt data with a customer managed KMS key in account B. What permissions are needed?",["Only an IAM policy in account A that allows kms:Decrypt on the key","Only a key policy in account B that names account A as a principal","A key policy in B granting account A, and an IAM policy in A","A multi-Region replica of the key created directly in account A"],2,"Cross-account KMS access needs both sides: the key policy grants the other account, and an IAM policy in that account grants the principal. Either alone is not enough.","Task 2.2",["Without the key policy in account B allowing account A, the IAM policy alone cannot grant access to a key in another account.","The key policy lets account A delegate access, but the function's role in account A still needs an IAM policy allowing kms:Decrypt.",null,"Multi-Region replica keys are created in other Regions of the same account, not in a different account."],3],["dva49",0,2,"Data must be encrypted inside the application before it is sent to any AWS service, so AWS never sees plaintext. What should the developer use?",["Client-side encryption with the AWS Encryption SDK","SSE-S3 default encryption configured on each bucket","SSE-KMS server-side encryption with an AWS managed key","TLS 1.2 or later enforced on every service connection"],0,"Client-side encryption encrypts data before it leaves the application. Server-side options encrypt after the service receives the data, and TLS protects data only in transit.","Task 2.2",[null,"SSE-S3 encrypts only after S3 receives the data, so AWS still sees the plaintext in transit to the service.","SSE-KMS is still server-side encryption; S3 receives the plaintext and encrypts it on arrival.","TLS protects data in transit only; the service decrypts it on arrival and sees plaintext."],1],["dva50",0,2,"Application logs in CloudWatch Logs sometimes contain customer email addresses. The team wants those values masked automatically. What should they use?",["A CloudWatch metric filter","A shorter log retention period","A subscription filter to Kinesis","A CloudWatch Logs data protection policy"],3,"Data protection policies detect and mask sensitive data such as email addresses in log events. Metric filters only count patterns, and shorter retention does not hide the data.","Task 2.3",["A metric filter counts or extracts values into metrics; it does not mask the email addresses in the log events.","Shorter retention deletes logs sooner but the emails remain visible until then.","A subscription filter streams log events elsewhere, copying the sensitive data rather than masking it.",null],1],["dva51",0,2,"A developer wants to replace a broad policy on a role with one that grants only the actions the application actually used in the last 90 days. Which tool helps generate it?",["AWS Trusted Advisor cost optimization checks","IAM Access Analyzer policy generation","Amazon Inspector vulnerability findings for the role","AWS Config conformance packs applied to the account"],1,"IAM Access Analyzer can review CloudTrail logs for a role and generate a policy with only the used actions. Inspector scans workloads for vulnerabilities, not permissions.","Task 2.1",["Trusted Advisor cost checks look for idle or underused resources, not unused IAM permissions.",null,"Inspector scans EC2, ECR images and Lambda for vulnerabilities; it does not generate IAM policies.","Config conformance packs evaluate resource configuration against rules; they do not build a policy from actual API usage."],2],["dva52",0,3,"A developer has an AWS SAM template and wants to build the code and deploy it for the first time, answering prompts for the stack name and Region. Which commands should be run?",["sam init, then sam local invoke","cdk synth, then cdk deploy","sam build, then sam deploy --guided","aws s3 cp, then aws lambda invoke"],2,"sam build prepares the artifacts and sam deploy --guided asks for settings and saves them to samconfig.toml for later runs. sam local invoke only runs a function locally.","Task 3.1",["sam init creates a new project from a template and sam local invoke runs a function locally; neither deploys to AWS.","cdk synth and cdk deploy belong to the AWS CDK, not to an existing SAM template.",null,"Copying files to S3 and invoking a function does not build or deploy the SAM stack at all."],1],["dva53",0,3,"A developer wants to run a Lambda function locally with a sample event before deploying it. Which tool supports this?",["aws lambda invoke against the $LATEST version","CloudFormation change sets","sam local invoke with an event file (using Docker)","CodeDeploy in-place deployment"],2,"SAM CLI emulates the Lambda runtime in a Docker container, so the function can be tested locally with a JSON event. aws lambda invoke calls the deployed function in AWS.","Task 3.2",["aws lambda invoke calls the function already deployed in AWS, so it cannot test code before it is deployed.","Change sets preview CloudFormation resource changes; they do not execute function code.",null,"CodeDeploy deploys code to AWS compute targets; it does not run a function locally for testing."],1],["dva54",0,3,"A CloudFormation template references local Lambda code folders. Before deploying with the AWS CLI, what must happen to that code?",["Paste the code inline into the ZipFile property of every function resource","Run aws cloudformation package to upload it to S3 and rewrite the paths","Store the code in Parameter Store and reference each parameter by name","Copy the code to an EC2 instance that CloudFormation reads it from"],1,"The package command zips local artifacts, uploads them to an S3 bucket and outputs a template with S3 locations for deploy to use.","Task 3.1",["Inline ZipFile code works only for small single-file functions (up to 4 KB) and is not how local code folders are packaged.",null,"Parameter Store holds configuration values, not deployment packages, and CloudFormation cannot load function code from it.","CloudFormation cannot read code from an EC2 instance; function code must come from S3, an image in ECR or inline."],2],["dva55",0,3,"A team deploys a new Lambda version and wants 10% of traffic to go to it for 5 minutes, then all traffic if no alarms fire, with automatic rollback. What should they use?",["CodeDeploy with Canary10Percent5Minutes on an alias","An all-at-once update of the function's $LATEST version","Two separate functions behind an ALB with equal target weights","A new Lambda layer that holds the new code for the function"],0,"CodeDeploy shifts alias weights between versions following the canary configuration and rolls back if CloudWatch alarms go off. Updating $LATEST changes all traffic at once.","Task 3.4",[null,"Updating $LATEST sends 100% of traffic to the new code at once with no canary period or automatic rollback.","Weighted ALB targets could split traffic, but there is no automatic 5-minute canary step or alarm-driven rollback.","A layer holds shared libraries; it does not control traffic between versions or roll back."],2],["dva56",0,3,"Which CodeDeploy traffic-shifting option for Lambda moves traffic in equal increments at fixed intervals, such as 10% every minute?",["Canary","All-at-once","Blue/green with CNAME swap","Linear"],3,"Linear shifts equal percentages at regular intervals until 100%. Canary shifts one first percentage, waits, then moves the rest.","Task 3.4",["Canary shifts one initial percentage, waits, then moves all remaining traffic at once, rather than equal steps.","All-at-once moves 100% of traffic immediately with no increments.","A CNAME swap is an Elastic Beanstalk blue/green technique, not a CodeDeploy traffic-shifting configuration for Lambda.",null],1],["dva57",0,3,"An Elastic Beanstalk production app must keep full capacity during deployments, and a failed deployment must roll back quickly by terminating the new instances. Which deployment policy fits?",["All at once","Immutable","Rolling","Rolling with an additional batch"],1,"Immutable deployments launch new instances in a temporary Auto Scaling group, so the old ones keep serving; rollback just terminates the new group. Rolling reduces capacity during the update.","Task 3.4",["All at once takes every instance out of service together, causing downtime and a slow redeploy to roll back.",null,"Rolling updates instances in batches, so capacity drops during deployment and rollback means redeploying.","Rolling with an additional batch keeps capacity, but it updates existing instances, so rollback requires another deployment rather than terminating new ones."],3],["dva58",0,3,"A developer wants the fastest possible Elastic Beanstalk deployment for a development environment and can accept brief downtime. Which policy should be used?",["Immutable","Traffic splitting","All at once","Rolling with an additional batch"],2,"All at once updates every instance at the same time, which is fastest but causes an outage while instances restart.","Task 3.4",["Immutable launches a whole new set of instances, which is safe but the slowest policy.","Traffic splitting runs new instances alongside old ones for an evaluation period, which takes longer.",null,"Rolling with an additional batch launches extra instances and updates in batches, which is slower than all at once."],1],["dva59",0,3,"A team runs version 2 in a separate Elastic Beanstalk environment and wants to cut over with almost no downtime and an easy way back. What should they do?",["Swap the environment URLs (CNAME swap) between the two environments","Deploy version 2 to the existing environment with the all-at-once policy","Delete the old environment and then rename the new one","Change the Auto Scaling group's minimum size to zero"],0,"A blue/green swap exchanges the CNAMEs so traffic moves to the new environment; swapping back restores the old one.","Task 3.4",[null,"An all-at-once deployment to the existing environment causes downtime and has no quick way back.","Deleting the old environment removes the easy rollback path, and environments cannot simply be renamed to take over the URL.","Setting minimum size to zero could shut down capacity; it does not move traffic to version 2."],2],["dva60",0,3,"A pipeline in CodePipeline must pause after deploying to staging until a team lead confirms the release. What should be added?",["A CodeBuild test action that runs automatically after the staging deploy","A CloudWatch alarm that stops the pipeline until it is cleared","A longer timeout on the staging deploy action in the pipeline","A manual approval action between the staging and production stages"],3,"Manual approval actions stop the pipeline until someone approves or rejects, optionally sending an SNS notification.","Task 3.4",["An automated test action runs without a person; it cannot wait for a team lead's confirmation.","CloudWatch alarms do not pause a pipeline for human sign-off; a manual approval action is the built-in way to do that.","A longer action timeout only lets the deployment run longer; it does not pause the pipeline for someone to approve.",null],2],["dva61",0,3,"In a CodeBuild buildspec.yml, where should unit tests that must run after the code compiles be placed?",["In the install phase runtime-versions section","In the build or post_build phase commands","In the artifacts files section","In the env variables section"],1,"Phases run in order: install, pre_build, build, post_build. Tests after compilation belong in build or post_build; artifacts only lists output files.","Task 3.3",["runtime-versions selects language runtimes during install, which happens before the code is compiled.",null,"The artifacts section lists output files to keep; it does not run commands.","The env section defines environment variables and runs no commands."],1],["dva62",0,3,"A CodeDeploy deployment to EC2 must run integration tests after the application has started. Which appspec.yml hook should run the test script?",["ValidateService","BeforeInstall","ApplicationStop","DownloadBundle"],0,"ValidateService is the last hook of an EC2/on-premises deployment and runs after ApplicationStart, making it the place to verify the service. DownloadBundle is reserved for the agent and cannot run scripts.","Task 3.3",[null,"BeforeInstall runs before the new files are installed, so the application is not running yet.","ApplicationStop runs first, to stop the old version, well before the new application starts.","DownloadBundle is run by the CodeDeploy agent itself and cannot run user scripts."],1],["dva63",0,3,"A CodeDeploy deployment to a new set of EC2 instances fails immediately with no lifecycle events recorded. What is the most likely cause?",["The revision bundle is stored in S3 instead of a GitHub repository","The CodeDeploy agent is not installed or not running on the instances","The deployment group selects the instances by EC2 tag instead of ASG","The appspec.yml file does not define a ValidateService lifecycle hook"],1,"On EC2 the CodeDeploy agent carries out deployments; without it, no lifecycle events run. Bundles in S3 and tag-based groups are normal.","Task 3.4",["Storing the revision in S3 is a normal, supported source for CodeDeploy and does not stop lifecycle events from running.",null,"Tag-based deployment groups are a normal way to target EC2 instances and do not prevent lifecycle events.","ValidateService is optional; leaving it out would not stop all lifecycle events from running."],3],["dva64",0,3,"An API Gateway API has dev and prod stages, and each stage must call a different alias of the same Lambda function. What should the developer use?",["A separate AWS account for each stage","Two different Lambda functions with identical code","Stage variables referenced in the Lambda integration ARN","Usage plans attached to each stage"],2,"The integration can reference ${stageVariables.alias}, so each stage calls its own alias. Remember to allow API Gateway to invoke each alias.","Task 3.4",["Separate accounts per stage would still leave each stage needing its own integration target; stage variables do this within one API.","Duplicating functions creates code drift and ignores aliases, which exist to point to versions of one function.",null,"Usage plans throttle and meter API keys per stage; they cannot change which Lambda alias the integration calls."],2],["dva65",0,3,"A developer changed several REST API methods in the API Gateway console, but clients still see the old behavior. What step was missed?",["Enabling CORS on the API","Creating a new usage plan","Deploying the API to the stage","Turning on stage caching"],2,"REST API changes take effect only after a deployment to a stage creates a new snapshot.","Task 3.4",["CORS controls which browser origins may call the API; it does not publish changed methods.","Usage plans manage throttling and quotas for API keys; they do not activate method changes.",null,"Stage caching could serve old responses, but it is off by default; the missing step is deploying the changes to the stage."],1],["dva66",0,3,"A Lambda function's dependencies add up to 1.5 GB, which is too large for a .zip deployment package. How can it still run on Lambda?",["Package it as a container image in Amazon ECR","Split the dependencies across six Lambda layers","Upload the .zip to S3 instead of directly","Store the libraries in environment variables"],0,"Container images up to 10 GB are supported. .zip packages, including layers, are limited to 250 MB unzipped, so extra layers do not help.","Task 3.1",[null,"Layers count toward the same 250 MB unzipped limit as the function package, so splitting across layers does not help.","Uploading via S3 raises the upload size limit, but the 250 MB unzipped limit still applies.","Environment variables are limited to 4 KB in total and cannot hold libraries."],2],["dva67",0,3,"Before updating a production CloudFormation stack, a developer wants to see exactly which resources will be modified or replaced. What should they use?",["Stack drift detection","A stack policy","Termination protection","A change set"],3,"A change set lists the proposed changes, including replacements, before you execute it. Drift detection compares current resources to the last deployed template instead.","Task 3.4",["Drift detection compares live resources with the last deployed template; it does not preview a new update.","A stack policy protects resources from being updated or replaced, but it does not show what a change will do.","Termination protection only prevents the stack from being deleted.",null],1],["dva68",0,3,"A networking stack creates a VPC, and application stacks must reference its subnet IDs without hardcoding them. What should the developer use?",["Fn::GetAtt with the networking stack's name to read its subnets","Subnet IDs copied into each application template's Mappings by hand","Outputs exported by the networking stack, read with Fn::ImportValue","A DependsOn attribute that points at the networking stack"],2,"Cross-stack references export an output from one stack and import it by name in another. Fn::GetAtt only works within one template.","Task 3.1",["Fn::GetAtt only reads attributes of resources in the same template; it cannot reach into another stack.","Copying IDs into Mappings is hardcoding, and the templates break whenever the networking stack changes.",null,"DependsOn only orders resource creation within one template; it cannot reference another stack or pass values."],3],["dva69",0,3,"A team defines infrastructure in TypeScript with the AWS CDK. Which command produces the CloudFormation template without deploying it?",["cdk bootstrap","cdk deploy","cdk destroy","cdk synth"],3,"cdk synth synthesizes the app into CloudFormation templates. cdk bootstrap creates resources the CDK needs in an account and Region.","Task 3.1",["cdk bootstrap provisions the staging resources (such as an S3 bucket and roles) the CDK needs in an account and Region.","cdk deploy synthesizes and then deploys the stacks, which the question wants to avoid.","cdk destroy deletes deployed stacks.",null],1],["dva70",0,3,"A front-end team needs an API endpoint that returns a fixed sample JSON response so they can build the UI before the backend exists. What is the quickest option?",["A Lambda function with provisioned concurrency","An Application Load Balancer with fixed targets","An API Gateway mock integration","A DynamoDB table with a sample item"],2,"Mock integrations return a response defined in API Gateway without calling any backend, which suits early testing.","Task 3.2",["A Lambda function still has to be written and deployed, and provisioned concurrency only cuts cold starts at extra cost.","An ALB fixed-response rule can return static text, but it adds an hourly-billed load balancer and is slower to set up than a mock integration in the API itself.",null,"A DynamoDB table is only storage; you would still need an API and code to return the item."],1],["dva71",0,3,"A team wants to roll out a configuration change to their application gradually, validate it against a schema, and roll back automatically if an alarm fires, without redeploying code. What should they use?",["A new Lambda version for each setting change","AWS AppConfig with a deployment strategy and validators","Environment variables updated with the CLI","A CloudFormation stack policy"],1,"AppConfig deploys configuration and feature flags with gradual strategies, validators and CloudWatch alarm rollback. Changing environment variables redeploys the function configuration all at once.","Task 3.4",["Publishing a new version for each setting change is a code deployment, with no schema validation or gradual rollout of the setting.",null,"Updating environment variables changes the configuration for all invocations at once, with no validator or alarm rollback.","A stack policy prevents updates to protected resources; it does not roll out configuration gradually."],2],["dva72",0,3,"Several teams want to host private npm and PyPI packages and proxy the public registries for their builds. Which service fits?",["AWS CodeArtifact","Amazon ECR","AWS CodeDeploy","Amazon S3 Glacier"],0,"CodeArtifact is a managed artifact repository for npm, PyPI, Maven and more, with upstream connections to public registries. ECR stores container images.","Task 3.1",[null,"ECR stores container images, not npm or PyPI packages, and does not proxy those public registries.","CodeDeploy deploys applications to compute targets; it is not a package repository.","S3 Glacier is archival storage with slow retrieval and no package-manager protocol support."],1],["dva73",0,4,"A developer uses X-Ray and wants to filter traces by customer tier (for example, tier = gold). Where should that value be recorded?",["As metadata on the segment","In the function's environment variables","As an annotation on the segment or subsegment","In the trace ID header"],2,"Annotations are indexed key-value pairs you can use in filter expressions. Metadata is stored with the trace but is not indexed for search.","Task 4.2",["Metadata is stored with the trace but is not indexed, so filter expressions cannot search on it.","Environment variables configure the function and are not recorded on traces, so X-Ray cannot filter by them.",null,"The trace ID header only propagates trace context between services; you cannot add custom values to filter by."],1],["dva74",0,4,"An application on EC2 is instrumented with the X-Ray SDK, but no traces appear in X-Ray. What is the most likely cause?",["The X-Ray daemon or agent is not running, or the role cannot write traces","Active tracing is not turned on for the instance in the Lambda console","The instance has no Elastic IP address, so traces cannot leave it","CloudTrail is disabled in the Region, so trace data is not collected"],0,"On EC2 the SDK sends segments over UDP to a local daemon or agent, which uploads them using the instance role. Lambda's active tracing setting does not apply to EC2.","Task 4.1",[null,"Active tracing is a Lambda setting; it does not apply to an EC2 instance.","Traces reach X-Ray through the daemon or agent using normal outbound connectivity; no Elastic IP is required.","CloudTrail records API activity and is unrelated to collecting X-Ray trace segments."],2],["dva75",0,4,"A Lambda function must publish custom business metrics to CloudWatch without making synchronous PutMetricData calls on every request. What should it use?",["A CloudWatch Logs subscription to Kinesis","The CloudWatch embedded metric format in its log output","An X-Ray sampling rule","CloudTrail data events"],1,"The embedded metric format writes structured JSON log lines that CloudWatch turns into metrics asynchronously, avoiding extra API calls and latency.","Task 4.2",["A subscription filter streams log events to Kinesis; it does not create CloudWatch metrics from them.",null,"Sampling rules decide which requests X-Ray traces; they do not publish business metrics.","CloudTrail data events log API calls for auditing and do not produce custom application metrics."],2],["dva76",0,4,"During an incident, a developer needs to find the 20 slowest requests from the last hour across many log streams. Which tool fits best?",["AWS Config","Amazon Inspector","IAM Access Analyzer","CloudWatch Logs Insights"],3,"Logs Insights runs queries (filter, stats, sort) across log groups, which suits finding the slowest requests. The other tools are for configuration, vulnerabilities and permissions.","Task 4.1",["AWS Config records resource configuration changes, not application request logs.","Inspector scans workloads for software vulnerabilities and cannot query log events.","Access Analyzer finds unintended resource access and generates policies; it does not search logs.",null],1],["dva77",0,4,"During a traffic spike, synchronous calls to a Lambda function fail with TooManyRequestsException (HTTP 429). What is the most likely cause?",["The function's memory is set too high","The deployment package is larger than 50 MB","The function has hit its concurrency limit","The function's execution role is missing a policy"],2,"429 throttling means no concurrency is available, either the account limit or the function's reserved concurrency. Request a quota increase or adjust reserved concurrency; a missing policy would cause AccessDenied instead.","Task 4.1",["High memory raises cost per millisecond but does not cause 429 throttling errors.","An oversized package fails at deployment time, not with runtime throttling during a traffic spike.",null,"A missing execution-role policy causes AccessDenied errors from the called service, not 429 from Lambda."],2],["dva78",0,4,"A CPU-heavy Lambda function takes 9 seconds at 512 MB of memory. What change is most likely to make it faster?",["Increase the memory setting, which also allocates more CPU","Increase the timeout so the function has more time to finish","Add reserved concurrency so each invocation gets a dedicated core","Move the function's code into a layer so it loads more quickly"],0,"Lambda allocates CPU in proportion to memory, so more memory often shortens CPU-bound runs and can even lower cost. A longer timeout does not add speed.","Task 4.3",[null,"A longer timeout only allows the function to run longer; it does not make the CPU-bound work any faster.","Reserved concurrency limits and guarantees the number of concurrent environments; it does not add CPU to each one.","Layers only package shared code; the code runs the same way and gets no extra CPU."],2],["dva79",0,4,"A latency-sensitive API backed by Lambda sees slow responses after quiet periods because new execution environments must start. What removes most of this delay?",["Provisioned concurrency on the function's alias","A larger /tmp storage setting","Reserved concurrency set to 0","A dead-letter queue"],0,"Provisioned concurrency keeps initialized environments ready, avoiding cold starts. Reserved concurrency of 0 would stop the function from running at all. Provisioned concurrency is billed while it is configured.","Task 4.3",[null,"Ephemeral /tmp storage size does not affect how long a new execution environment takes to initialize.","Reserved concurrency of 0 throttles every invocation, so the function would not run at all.","A DLQ captures failed asynchronous events; it does nothing about cold-start latency."],2],["dva80",0,4,"A DynamoDB table using the date as its partition key throttles writes even though total consumed capacity is well below the table's limit. What is the likely cause and fix?",["The table needs a local secondary index on the date attribute","A hot partition; use a partition key with many distinct values","Strongly consistent reads are enabled on the table's queries","The TTL attribute is missing, so old items are never removed"],1,"All of today's writes land on one partition key value, concentrating traffic. A high-cardinality key (or adding a random suffix) spreads writes across partitions.","Task 4.3",["An LSI must be defined at table creation, shares the same partition key, and adds write load rather than spreading it.",null,"Strongly consistent reads affect read capacity, not write throttling on one partition key value.","TTL deletes expired items but does not spread today's writes across partitions."],3],["dva81",0,4,"A REST API returns the same product catalog to thousands of clients, and each request invokes Lambda and reads DynamoDB. How can the developer reduce backend calls with minimal code?",["Increase the Lambda function's memory","Switch DynamoDB to on-demand capacity","Add a dead-letter queue to the function","Enable API Gateway stage caching with a suitable TTL"],3,"Stage caching returns cached responses for the TTL without invoking the backend. Note that the cache is billed by the hour and is not part of the Free Tier.","Task 4.3",["More memory makes each invocation faster but every request still invokes Lambda and reads DynamoDB.","On-demand capacity changes how DynamoDB is billed and scaled but does not reduce the number of backend calls.","A DLQ captures failed async events and does not reduce requests for the same catalog.",null],2],["dva82",0,4,"Users around the world download static images from an S3 bucket and see high latency. What is the best improvement?",["Enable S3 versioning","Serve the bucket through a CloudFront distribution","Move the images to DynamoDB","Enable S3 Object Lock"],1,"CloudFront caches content at edge locations close to users. Versioning and Object Lock protect data but do not reduce latency.","Task 4.3",["Versioning keeps previous object versions for recovery; it does nothing for download latency.",null,"DynamoDB has a 400 KB item limit and is not suited to serving images, nor would it be closer to users.","Object Lock enforces WORM retention; it does not cache content near users."],1],["dva83",0,4,"A malformed message in an SQS queue keeps failing and being retried, blocking attention from other work. How should the developer isolate such messages?",["Configure a dead-letter queue with a maxReceiveCount","Shorten the queue's message retention period to 1 minute","Turn off long polling so consumers poll more frequently","Increase the batch size to 10 so bad messages are diluted"],0,"A redrive policy moves a message to the DLQ after it has been received maxReceiveCount times, so it can be inspected without endless retries.","Task 4.1",[null,"A 1-minute retention period would delete all slow or unprocessed messages, not just the malformed one.","Turning off long polling only increases empty receives and cost; the bad message still keeps failing.","A larger batch does not isolate the bad message and can cause more good messages to be retried alongside it."],2],["dva84",0,4,"A Lambda function reading a Kinesis data stream shows a steadily rising IteratorAge metric. What does this indicate, and what helps?",["Records are expiring early; reduce the retention period","The function is over-provisioned; lower its memory","The function is falling behind; raise the parallelization factor or add shards","The stream is empty; add a producer"],2,"A growing iterator age means records wait longer before processing. Processing more batches in parallel per shard, adding shards or speeding up the code reduces it.","Task 4.1",["Records expire only after the retention period, and a shorter period would make lagging records expire and be lost.","Lowering memory would make the function slower and increase the lag further.",null,"An empty stream would produce no iterator age growth; a rising age means records are waiting unprocessed."],3],["dva85",0,4,"A developer wants an email when a function's error count exceeds 5 in 5 minutes. What should they configure?",["A CloudWatch alarm on the Errors metric that notifies an SNS email topic","A CloudTrail trail that sends an email for every Lambda API call","An X-Ray group with a filter expression that matches failed traces","A Lambda destination that fires for every successful invocation"],0,"CloudWatch alarms watch a metric against a threshold and can send notifications through SNS.","Task 4.2",[null,"CloudTrail logs Lambda API calls such as function updates, not invocation errors, and emailing every call is not a threshold alert.","An X-Ray group filters traces, but by itself it does not send an email when errors pass a threshold over 5 minutes.","A destination for successful invocations fires on successes, not on an error count threshold."],1],["dva86",0,4,"Requests pass through API Gateway, two Lambda functions and SQS. When a user reports an error, engineers cannot connect the log lines from each component. What logging practice helps most?",["Longer log retention settings on every component's log group","Structured JSON logs carrying a correlation ID for each request","Writing every component's logs to its local /tmp folder instead","Logging only errors and dropping all informational log messages"],1,"A shared correlation (or trace) ID in structured logs lets you search every component for one request, for example with Logs Insights.","Task 4.2",["Longer retention keeps logs around longer but does not link log lines from different components to one request.",null,"/tmp is ephemeral storage local to one execution environment, so logs there are lost and not searchable.","Dropping informational logs removes context and still gives no way to tie events for one request together."],2],["dva87",0,4,"An API Gateway REST API calls a Lambda function that sometimes needs 2 minutes to generate a report, and clients get 504 errors. What is the best design change?",["Raise the Lambda timeout to 15 minutes","Increase the Lambda memory to the maximum","Return a job ID right away and process the report asynchronously","Enable caching on the API stage"],2,"REST API integrations time out at 29 seconds by default, so a 504 occurs no matter how long Lambda may run. An asynchronous job pattern avoids holding the request open.","Task 4.3",["Lambda can run for 15 minutes, but the API Gateway integration still times out at 29 seconds by default and returns 504.","More memory may speed the report up, but a 2-minute job is unlikely to fit within the 29-second integration timeout.",null,"Caching returns stored responses for repeat requests; the first request for each report still times out."],3],["dva88",0,4,"A Lambda function fails with AccessDeniedException when calling s3:GetObject on a bucket in the same account. The bucket has no policy. Where should the developer look first?",["The function's resource-based policy","The function's reserved concurrency","The bucket's CORS configuration","The function's execution role policy"],3,"Calls the function makes are authorized by its execution role. The resource-based policy controls who can invoke the function, and CORS only affects browsers.","Task 4.1",["The resource-based policy controls who can invoke the function, not what the function can access.","Reserved concurrency limits how many copies run; it does not cause AccessDeniedException.","CORS only governs browser cross-origin requests; it does not authorize SDK calls from Lambda.",null],3],["dva89",0,4,"A read-heavy app sends the same expensive SQL queries to an RDS database many times per second, and the results change rarely. What should the developer add?",["A larger RDS instance storage volume","An Amazon ElastiCache cache using lazy loading with a TTL","A CloudWatch Logs subscription filter","A second NAT gateway"],1,"Caching query results in ElastiCache cuts repeated database reads, and a TTL limits how stale results can get.","Task 4.3",["More storage adds capacity for data but does not reduce the repeated query load on the database.",null,"A subscription filter streams logs elsewhere and has no effect on database query load.","A NAT gateway provides outbound internet access for private subnets and does not cache or reduce database queries."],2],["x1",0,1,"A team must process tens of thousands of IoT events per second with AWS Step Functions. Each run finishes in well under a minute, and low cost per execution matters more than a long, exactly-once execution history. Which workflow type fits?",["Standard workflows, because they keep a full execution history for every run","Express workflows, built for high-volume, short-duration event processing","Standard workflows with a Wait state that groups events into batches first","Activity tasks polled by EC2 workers from inside a Standard workflow"],1,"Express workflows are designed for high event rates and short runs (up to five minutes) and are priced for volume. Standard workflows give exactly-once execution with long retained history, which this scenario says it does not need.","Domain 1: Step Functions Standard vs Express",["Standard workflows suit long-running, exactly-once orchestration; at this event rate and short duration they cost more than Express.",null,"A Wait state only pauses a run; it does not batch events and still leaves every execution as a costlier Standard run.","Activity workers add servers to manage and do nothing to address the per-execution cost of a Standard workflow."],2],["x2",0,1,"A Step Functions Task state calls a Lambda function that sometimes fails with a transient error. The workflow must retry a few times with growing delays and, if it still fails, move to a cleanup state. What should the developer add to the Task state?",["A Choice state after the task that loops back when output has an error","A Parallel state running the same task twice, keeping the branch that works","A Wait state before the task so the downstream service can recover first","A Retry field with a BackoffRate, and a Catch field routing to cleanup"],3,"Retry with IntervalSeconds, MaxAttempts and BackoffRate gives bounded exponential retries, and Catch sends any remaining failure to a recovery state. A Choice loop is the tempting DIY option but lacks backoff and cannot catch task errors.","Domain 1: Step Functions retry and catch",["A hand-built Choice loop reinvents Retry, has no built-in backoff, and never catches errors that stop the Task state outright.","Running the task twice in parallel doubles the side effects and gives no bounded retries or failure routing.","A fixed Wait before the first attempt does not retry anything after a failure and adds delay to every successful run.",null],2],["x3",0,1,"A script calls DynamoDB Scan through the SDK and processes only part of a large table. The response contains a LastEvaluatedKey value. What is happening, and what should the script do?",["The 1 MB page limit was hit; call Scan again with ExclusiveStartKey","The table throttled the call and returned partial data instead of an error","Eventually consistent reads hid some items; retry with ConsistentRead","The filter removed the other items; the key marks the final match found"],0,"Scan and Query return at most 1 MB per call. LastEvaluatedKey tells the caller to request the next page by passing it as ExclusiveStartKey (or to use the SDK paginator). Throttling looks different: it raises an exception.","Domain 1: SDK pagination",[null,"Throttling returns a ProvisionedThroughputExceededException, not a partial page with a LastEvaluatedKey.","Consistency affects how fresh items are, not how many are returned; a LastEvaluatedKey means more pages remain.","A FilterExpression is applied after each page is read; LastEvaluatedKey signals that more of the table is left to scan."],2],["x4",0,1,"Right after calling CreateTable with the SDK, a script writes items and fails because the table is still being created. What is the cleanest fix?",["Retry each write in a tight loop every few milliseconds until it works","Switch the table to on-demand capacity so it becomes active at once","Use the SDK waiter for table existence before writing any items","Call DescribeTable once and start writing whatever status it returns"],2,"SDK waiters (such as TableExists / table_exists) poll DescribeTable until the table is ACTIVE, with delays and a maximum attempts limit. A hand-written tight loop is tempting but hammers the API and has no backoff.","Domain 1: SDK waiters",["A tight retry loop wastes calls and can hit throttling; waiters poll at sensible intervals with a limit.","Capacity mode does not skip table creation; the table still passes through CREATING before it is ACTIVE.",null,"Checking once without acting on the status does nothing to wait for ACTIVE, so writes can still fail."],1],["x5",0,1,"An EC2 instance has an instance profile role, but a developer also exported AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID and AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY for an IAM user in the shell. The application's SDK calls run with the IAM user's permissions. Why?",["The instance profile is ignored whenever the instance has a public IPv4 address","IAM always prefers IAM user permissions over role permissions when both exist","The default credential chain checks environment variables before the instance role","The role credentials expired, so the SDK fell back to the environment variables"],2,"The SDK default provider chain finds explicit and environment-variable credentials before it reaches the instance metadata role, so leftover keys override the role. Removing the variables lets the instance profile be used. Expired role credentials is tempting, but the SDK renews them.","Domain 1: SDK credential provider chain",["A public IP has no effect on whether the SDK reads instance metadata credentials.","IAM evaluates the single principal making the call; it never merges or prefers users over roles.",null,"The SDK refreshes role credentials automatically; the env variables win because they come earlier in the chain."],3],["x6",0,1,"A Lambda function downloads a 2 GB model file from S3 on each cold start and fails with 'No space left on device'. Memory and timeout are fine. What should the developer change?",["Raise the function's ephemeral storage (/tmp) setting","Raise the function timeout to the 15-minute maximum allowed","Put the model file into an environment variable","Turn on provisioned concurrency for the alias"],0,"Lambda's /tmp defaults to 512 MB and can be configured larger (up to 10,240 MB). A file bigger than the default needs more ephemeral storage. Provisioned concurrency is tempting because it reduces cold starts, but it does not change disk size.","Domain 1: Lambda ephemeral storage",[null,"The error is about disk space, not time; a longer timeout still leaves /tmp too small.","Environment variables are limited to a few kilobytes in total and cannot hold a large binary file.","Provisioned concurrency keeps environments warm but does not enlarge their /tmp storage."],2],["x7",0,1,"An asynchronously invoked Lambda function must send a record of every successful invocation, including the function's response, to an audit function without changing the handler code. What should be configured?",["A dead-letter queue attached to the function for async events","A CloudWatch Logs subscription on the log group","Reserved concurrency on the audit function","An on-success destination for asynchronous invocations"],3,"Lambda destinations for asynchronous invocations can send an invocation record, including the response payload, to a target on success or on failure. A DLQ is the tempting choice but only captures failed events and holds the event, not the result.","Domain 1: Lambda async invocation destinations",["A dead-letter queue receives only events that failed all retries, never successful results.","Log subscriptions forward log lines, not the invocation record with the returned payload, and need parsing.","Reserved concurrency limits scaling of the audit function; it does not route anything to it.",null],2],["x8",0,1,"A Lambda function reads a DynamoDB stream. One malformed record makes every batch fail, and Lambda keeps retrying that shard, so newer changes on the shard are stuck. Which event source mapping changes address this best?",["Raise the batch size so the bad record is mixed in with more good records","Enable bisect on error, cap retry attempts, and add an on-failure destination","Lower the function's memory so failed invocations end sooner and cost less","Change the stream view type from NEW_AND_OLD_IMAGES to KEYS_ONLY"],1,"For stream sources, BisectBatchOnFunctionError splits a failing batch to isolate the bad record, MaximumRetryAttempts (or record age) stops endless retries, and an on-failure destination keeps details of the skipped record. Bigger batches just make every batch fail.","Domain 1: Lambda event source mappings for streams",["A bigger batch still fails as a whole whenever it contains the bad record, so the shard stays blocked.",null,"Less memory does not stop the retries; the same record keeps failing and blocking the shard.","The view type only changes what data each record carries; the malformed record would still fail processing."],3],["x9",0,1,"A producer sends messages to an SQS FIFO queue and sometimes repeats a SendMessage call after a network timeout, creating duplicates. Retried messages have identical bodies. What is the simplest way to prevent the duplicates?",["Enable content-based deduplication on the queue","Give every message its own unique MessageGroupId","Enable long polling with a 20-second wait time","Set DelaySeconds to zero on every send call"],0,"With content-based deduplication, SQS FIFO derives the deduplication ID from a SHA-256 hash of the body, so identical retries inside the deduplication interval are dropped. MessageGroupId is tempting because it is FIFO-specific, but it governs ordering.","Domain 1: SQS FIFO deduplication",[null,"The message group controls ordering and parallelism, not deduplication; duplicates would still be accepted.","Long polling changes how consumers receive, not whether the queue accepts a repeated send.","Delay settings only postpone delivery; they do nothing to recognize a repeated message."],2],["x10",0,1,"A Kinesis data stream has 10 shards. One shard returns ProvisionedThroughputExceededException while the others sit idle. Every producer uses the constant string 'events' as its partition key. What should the developer do?",["Increase the stream's data retention period","Switch the consumer applications to enhanced fan-out","Use a high-cardinality partition key such as the device ID","Merge shards so that throughput is concentrated in fewer shards"],2,"The partition key is hashed to choose a shard, so one constant key sends every record to one shard. A key with many distinct values spreads writes evenly. Enhanced fan-out is tempting but addresses consumer reads, not producer hot spots.","Domain 1: Kinesis partition keys",["Retention decides how long records are kept, not how writes are spread across shards.","Enhanced fan-out improves read throughput for consumers; the problem here is write skew from producers.",null,"Merging shards lowers total capacity and does nothing to spread one constant key across shards."],2],["x11",0,1,"Five independent applications read the same Kinesis data stream and compete for each shard's shared read throughput, so their processing latency grows. What should the developer use?",["Put an SQS queue between the stream and every app","Register each app as an enhanced fan-out consumer","Raise the retention period on the Kinesis stream","Raise the batch size used on each GetRecords call"],1,"Enhanced fan-out gives every registered consumer its own dedicated read throughput per shard, pushed over HTTP/2, so consumers stop competing. Bigger GetRecords batches are tempting, but they still share one per-shard limit.","Domain 1: Kinesis enhanced fan-out",["Adding queues creates extra plumbing and cost and still leaves something reading the shared shard throughput.",null,"Longer retention keeps records longer but does not give readers more throughput.","Larger batches per call still draw from the same shared per-shard read limit."],2],["x12",0,1,"Microservices publish events to a custom EventBridge event bus. A Lambda function must run only for events with source 'com.shop.orders' and detail-type 'OrderCancelled'. What should the developer create?",["An SNS filter policy on a topic subscribed to the default event bus","A function that receives every event and returns early for the rest","A CloudWatch Logs metric filter that looks for the event text","A rule on the custom bus whose event pattern matches source and detail-type"],3,"EventBridge rules use event patterns to match fields such as source and detail-type, then invoke only the matching targets. Filtering inside the function is the tempting shortcut, but it pays for every unneeded invocation.","Domain 1: EventBridge rules",["SNS filter policies apply to SNS subscriptions; the events here are on a custom EventBridge bus, and the default bus is the wrong bus.","Receiving every event works but wastes invocations and cost when EventBridge can filter before invoking.","Metric filters count log text; they do not route events to a Lambda function.",null],2],["x13",0,1,"An order process spans payment, inventory and shipping services. The team needs one place to see each order's current step, run compensating actions when a step fails, and review execution history. Which approach fits best?",["Choreography, with each service reacting to the others' EventBridge events","Synchronous HTTP calls chained one after another from the payment service","A shared DynamoDB table that each service polls to discover status changes","Orchestration with a Step Functions state machine calling each service"],3,"Orchestration puts the flow in one place: Step Functions shows each execution's state and history, and Catch paths can run compensating steps. Choreography is a valid pattern and tempting for decoupling, but it lacks a central view of each order.","Domain 1: Orchestration vs choreography",["Choreography keeps services loosely coupled but spreads the flow across services, so there is no single view of progress or central compensation.","Chained synchronous calls couple the services tightly and give no durable history or built-in error handling.","Polling a shared table couples services through data, adds latency, and still leaves no central flow or history.",null],3],["x14",0,1,"Placing an order must decrement stock in an Inventory table and insert a row into an Orders table. Both writes must succeed together or not at all, and stock must never go negative. What should the developer use?",["BatchWriteItem containing both requests in one call to DynamoDB","TransactWriteItems with a condition check that stock is sufficient","Two separate PutItem calls, retrying either one if it fails","A DynamoDB stream on Orders that updates the Inventory table afterwards"],1,"DynamoDB transactions apply all actions atomically across tables, and a ConditionExpression can cancel the whole transaction when stock is too low. BatchWriteItem is tempting because it groups writes, but it is not all-or-nothing.","Domain 1: DynamoDB transactions",["BatchWriteItem is not atomic: some requests can succeed while others fail, and it does not support condition expressions.",null,"Separate calls can leave one write applied and the other not if the process fails between them.","A stream update happens later and separately, so the two tables can disagree and stock checks are not enforced."],2],["x15",0,1,"A developer needs an alternate sort key on an existing, populated DynamoDB table (same partition key) and requires strongly consistent queries on it. What is the constraint?",["An LSI can be added any time but only supports eventually consistent reads","A GSI allows strongly consistent reads if it reuses the table's partition key","LSIs can only be defined at table creation, so a new table is required","Neither index type may use a sort key different from the base table's"],2,"A local secondary index shares the partition key, allows strongly consistent reads, and must be created with the table. A GSI can be added later but reads from it are eventually consistent, so it cannot meet the strong-consistency requirement.","Domain 1: DynamoDB LSI vs GSI",["It is the reverse: an LSI supports strongly consistent reads but cannot be added to an existing table.","GSIs support only eventually consistent reads, whatever key they use.",null,"Both index types exist precisely to provide a different sort key (or key schema) from the base table."],3],["x16",0,1,"A user saves a profile change and immediately reloads the page, but GetItem sometimes returns the old value. Which change makes GetItem return the latest committed data?",["Set ConsistentRead to true on the GetItem request","Add a global secondary index keyed on the profile ID attribute","Enable DynamoDB Streams on the profiles table","Place DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) in front of it"],0,"GetItem is eventually consistent by default. Setting ConsistentRead to true returns the most recent successful write. DAX is tempting as a performance fix, but its item cache serves eventually consistent results.","Domain 1: DynamoDB read consistency",[null,"GSIs are eventually consistent and would not help GetItem on the base table return fresher data.","Streams capture changes for other consumers; they do not change what a read returns.","DAX caches eventually consistent reads, so it can serve stale data rather than fixing it."],1],["x17",0,1,"Invoice PDFs in S3 are read often for 30 days, rarely afterwards, and must be deleted after 7 years. What is the lowest-effort way to manage them?",["A nightly Lambda that copies old objects to another bucket","Lifecycle rules: transition after 30 days, expire after 7 years","Versioning with MFA Delete turned on for the invoice bucket","Event notifications that tag objects once they are 30 days old"],1,"S3 Lifecycle configurations can transition objects to lower-cost storage classes after a set age and expire them later, with no code. A scheduled Lambda job is the tempting DIY answer, but it adds code and operations for a built-in feature.","Domain 1: S3 storage classes and lifecycle",["Custom copy code must be written, run and monitored, while lifecycle rules do this natively.",null,"Versioning and MFA Delete protect against deletion; they neither move data to cheaper storage nor expire it.","Tags alone change nothing; something else would still have to move and delete the objects."],1],["x18",0,1,"Several teams each need their own Lambda function to react to every new object in one bucket, and more consumers will be added over time without editing the bucket's configuration each time. What design fits best?",["Add one event notification per function, all using the same prefix","Have each function list the bucket every minute for new objects","Replicate the bucket to a separate copy owned by each team","Send S3 events to EventBridge and give each consumer its own rule"],3,"With EventBridge enabled on the bucket, every object event goes to the default bus, and each team adds its own rule and target without touching the bucket. Separate S3 notifications per function is tempting but fails for overlapping event types and prefixes.","Domain 1: S3 event notifications",["S3 does not allow overlapping notification configurations for the same event type and prefix, and each change edits the bucket.","Listing on a schedule is slow, costly at scale, and misses the point of event-driven processing.","Replication multiplies storage cost and still needs notifications on every copy.",null],3],["x19",0,1,"An application uses lazy loading with ElastiCache for Redis. After prices change in the database, users keep seeing old prices for hours. What is the most appropriate fix?",["Give cached keys a TTL, and delete or update keys when the data is written","Move to a Memcached cluster with more nodes in it","Choose a larger node type so more keys fit in memory","Enable Multi-AZ with automatic failover on the cluster"],0,"Lazy loading only refreshes a key on a cache miss, so entries without a TTL can stay stale indefinitely. A TTL bounds staleness, and invalidating or writing through on updates removes it. Bigger nodes are tempting but do not affect freshness.","Domain 1: ElastiCache caching strategies",[null,"Changing engine or node count adds capacity but keeps serving the same stale entries.","More memory just keeps stale keys around longer; freshness is the problem.","Multi-AZ improves availability, not how quickly cached values reflect database changes."],2],["x20",0,2,"A mobile app uses a Cognito user pool. Users should stay signed in for weeks without re-entering passwords, while ID and access tokens stay short-lived. What should the app do?",["Set the access token lifetime to several weeks long","Store the password on the device and sign in silently","Use the refresh token to get new ID and access tokens","Swap the ID token for an API Gateway API key"],2,"Cognito issues a refresh token alongside the ID and access tokens; the app uses it to obtain fresh short-lived tokens without prompting the user. Lengthening the access token is tempting but weakens security.","Domain 2: Cognito user pool tokens",["Long-lived access tokens widen the damage if one is stolen, which is exactly what short lifetimes are meant to avoid.","Storing passwords on devices is insecure and unnecessary when refresh tokens exist.",null,"API keys identify callers for usage plans; they are not user credentials or session tokens."],1],["x21",0,2,"A company sells access to its REST API and must give each customer a monthly request quota and a throttle rate. What should the developer configure in API Gateway?",["A Cognito authorizer with a group per customer","Resource policies listing customer IP ranges","API keys associated with usage plans","Stage variables holding each customer's limits"],2,"Usage plans define throttling and quotas, and API keys identify which customer a request counts against. AWS advises not to rely on API keys alone for authorization, so pair them with an authorizer. Cognito groups are tempting but do not meter usage.","Domain 2: API keys and usage plans",["Cognito groups control identity and authorization, not per-customer quotas and throttles.","Resource policies allow or deny access; they have no concept of monthly quotas.",null,"Stage variables pass configuration to integrations; API Gateway does not enforce limits from them."],1],["x22",0,2,"A token-based Lambda authorizer runs on every API request, adding latency and cost, even though a token's policy stays valid for several minutes. What should the developer do?",["Enable authorizer result caching with a suitable TTL","Enable API Gateway stage caching for all methods","Give the authorizer function more memory","Replace the authorizer with an API key required on each method"],0,"API Gateway can cache the policy a Lambda authorizer returns, keyed on the token (or identity sources), for a configured TTL, so repeat requests skip the authorizer. Stage caching sounds similar but applies to backend responses.","Domain 2: Lambda authorizers",[null,"Stage caching caches integration responses, not authorizer decisions, and could serve one user's data to another.","More memory may shave a little time but the authorizer still runs on every request.","API keys are not an authentication mechanism and would drop the token validation entirely."],2],["x23",0,2,"A role in account B was created for an app in account A. The app's IAM policy allows sts:AssumeRole on that role's ARN, but the call returns AccessDenied. What is most likely missing?",["The role has no session policy passed along with the AssumeRole call","Account A must first activate STS endpoints in the account's Region","The call must be made with the root user of account A to cross accounts","The role's trust policy does not name account A or its role as a principal"],3,"Cross-account role assumption needs both sides: an identity policy in A that allows sts:AssumeRole, and a trust policy on B's role that trusts A. With the caller's side already allowed, the missing trust policy is the likely cause.","Domain 2: STS AssumeRole and trust policies",["Session policies are optional and can only narrow permissions; their absence never blocks AssumeRole.","Regional STS endpoints are active by default in commercial Regions, and the error points to authorization, not an endpoint.","Using the root user is bad practice and unnecessary; a properly trusted IAM principal can assume the role.",null],2],["x24",0,2,"A company must keep its encryption keys outside AWS entirely but still wants Amazon S3 to perform the encryption and decryption. Which option fits?",["SSE-KMS with a customer managed key that the company controls","SSE-C, providing the key with each request over HTTPS","SSE-S3 using S3 managed keys","S3 Bucket Keys with SSE-KMS"],1,"With SSE-C the client supplies the key on every request; S3 uses it to encrypt or decrypt and does not store it. Customer managed KMS keys are tempting because the customer controls them, but they still reside in AWS.","Domain 2: S3 encryption options",["Customer managed KMS keys are stored and used inside AWS KMS, which the requirement rules out.",null,"SSE-S3 keys are created and held entirely by AWS.","Bucket Keys only reduce KMS calls for SSE-KMS; the keys still live in KMS."],1],["x25",0,2,"Objects in a bucket use SSE-KMS with the AWS managed aws/s3 key. The team now needs to edit the key policy and let another AWS account decrypt the data. What should they do?",["Create a customer managed key and encrypt with SSE-KMS using it","Edit the key policy of the aws/s3 AWS managed key directly","Switch the bucket's default encryption to SSE-S3","Enable S3 Bucket Keys on the aws/s3 AWS managed key"],0,"Only customer managed KMS keys let you write the key policy, grant other accounts, and control rotation. AWS managed keys such as aws/s3 are convenient but their policies are fixed, which is why editing them is not possible.","Domain 2: KMS customer managed vs AWS managed keys",[null,"AWS managed key policies cannot be edited, so they cannot grant another account access.","SSE-S3 has no key policy at all, so there would be nothing to grant or control.","Bucket Keys reduce KMS request volume; they do not change who controls the key policy."],2],["x26",0,2,"An application needs to store a 6 KB configuration document in Systems Manager Parameter Store and have it expire automatically through a parameter policy. Which option works?",["A standard-tier SecureString parameter","A standard-tier StringList parameter","An advanced-tier parameter","A standard-tier String with a label"],2,"Advanced-tier parameters allow values up to 8 KB and support parameter policies such as Expiration. Standard parameters max out at 4 KB and have no policies. SecureString is tempting because it sounds more capable, but it is a type, not a tier.","Domain 2: Parameter Store tiers",["Standard parameters are limited to 4 KB and cannot use parameter policies, whatever their type.","StringList is still a standard parameter, so the size limit and lack of policies remain.",null,"Labels are just version aliases; they neither raise the size limit nor add expiration."],2],["x27",0,2,"Users get AWS credentials through a Cognito identity pool. Each user must read and write only objects under an S3 prefix that matches their own identity ID. What is the best approach?",["A separate IAM role created for every user at the moment they sign up","One authenticated role whose policy uses the cognito-identity sub variable in the ARN","A bucket policy that lists every user's identity ARN one by one","Presigned URLs for all objects, regenerated once a day for each user"],1,"IAM policy variables such as ${cognito-identity.amazonaws.com:sub} resolve per request, so one role can restrict every user to their own prefix. Per-user roles look precise but do not scale.","Domain 2: Least privilege with Cognito identity pools",["Per-user roles do not scale and quickly run into IAM quotas and management overhead.",null,"A bucket policy listing each user grows without bound and must be edited on every sign-up.","Daily presigned URLs grant broad, long-lived access and do not scope uploads to each user's own prefix."],3],["x28",0,2,"A Lambda function reads a database secret from Secrets Manager on every invocation, adding latency and API calls. The secret is rotated periodically. What is the best practice?",["Copy the secret value into an environment variable at deploy time","Bundle the secret value into the function's deployment package","Turn off rotation so the value can be cached for as long as needed","Cache the secret outside the handler and refresh it periodically"],3,"Caching the secret in the execution environment (for example with a caching client or the Parameters and Secrets Lambda extension) with a refresh interval cuts calls while still picking up rotated values. An environment variable is tempting but becomes stale.","Domain 2: Secrets Manager in Lambda",["A deploy-time copy goes stale on the next rotation and exposes the value in function configuration.","Secrets in the deployment package are exposed to anyone who can download the code and never rotate.","Disabling rotation trades away a security control to fix a performance problem that caching solves.",null],2],["x29",0,2,"A REST API must be reachable only from one VPC through an interface VPC endpoint for API Gateway. What should the developer configure?",["A required API key on every method of the API","A Cognito user pool authorizer on each method","A usage plan that applies a very low throttle limit to all clients","A private API with a resource policy allowing that endpoint"],3,"Private REST APIs are reachable only through interface VPC endpoints, and a resource policy using aws:SourceVpce (or aws:SourceVpc) limits access to the approved endpoint. A Cognito authorizer is tempting but controls who, not from where.","Domain 2: API Gateway resource policies",["API keys are not an access control boundary and do nothing to restrict network origin.","A Cognito authorizer checks user identity but leaves the API reachable from anywhere.","Throttling limits request rate; it does not restrict where requests come from.",null],2],["x30",0,2,"A role's identity policy allows dynamodb:PutItem on a table, but the permissions boundary attached to the role allows only dynamodb:GetItem. What happens when the role calls PutItem?",["Allowed, because identity policies outrank permissions boundaries","Denied, because the effective permissions are the overlap of both","Allowed, but only if the table also has a resource-based policy","Denied only for items larger than the item size limit of the table"],1,"A permissions boundary caps what identity policies can grant: an action must be allowed by both. PutItem is outside the boundary, so it is implicitly denied. Thinking the identity policy wins is the common mistake.","Domain 2: IAM policy evaluation",["Boundaries are not outranked; they set the maximum an identity policy can grant.",null,"For same-account access, a resource policy cannot widen a role past its permissions boundary here.","Item size is irrelevant to IAM authorization; the action is simply not permitted."],3],["x31",0,2,"A game lets players try it without signing in, and these guests need limited temporary AWS credentials to write scores to a DynamoDB table. What should the developer enable?",["A Cognito user pool app client created without a client secret","One IAM user whose access keys are shared by all guest players","Guest (unauthenticated) identities in a Cognito identity pool","A resource policy on the table that allows public anonymous writes"],2,"Identity pools can issue temporary credentials to unauthenticated identities, mapped to a tightly scoped guest role. The user pool option is tempting because it is Cognito too, but user pools authenticate users rather than vend AWS credentials.","Domain 2: Cognito identity pools",["A user pool app client handles sign-in for registered users; it does not hand out AWS credentials to guests.","Shared long-term keys embedded in a client can be extracted and abused, and cannot be scoped per player.",null,"Allowing anonymous public writes removes all control over who can change the table."],2],["x32",0,2,"A presigned GET URL generated inside a Lambda function stops working long before the expiry time the code requested. What is the most likely reason?",["It was signed with the role's temporary credentials, which expired sooner","Presigned URLs work only for PutObject requests, never for GetObject","The bucket's default encryption is SSE-S3, which blocks presigned access","The object is stored in the S3 Standard class, which limits URL lifetime"],0,"A presigned URL is valid only while the credentials that signed it are valid. Lambda execution role credentials are temporary, so a URL can expire when they do, even if a longer expiry was requested. Blaming encryption is a tempting but wrong guess.","Domain 2: Presigned URLs",[null,"Presigned URLs support GET as well as PUT and other operations.","SSE-S3 is transparent to authorized requests, including presigned ones.","Storage class has no effect on how long a presigned URL is valid."],3],["x33",0,2,"A developer wants each KMS decrypt operation to fail unless the caller supplies the same tenant ID that was used at encryption time, and wants the tenant ID recorded in CloudTrail. What should they use?",["A data key cached separately for every tenant","An encryption context that includes the tenant ID","A separate key alias created for every tenant in the account","An unconstrained grant to each tenant's role"],1,"An encryption context is non-secret additional authenticated data: the same context must be supplied to decrypt, and it appears in CloudTrail logs. Per-tenant aliases are tempting but only name keys.","Domain 2: KMS encryption context",["Caching data keys per tenant does not make decryption depend on presenting a tenant ID.",null,"Aliases are friendly names for keys; they do not bind ciphertext to a tenant value.","A grant without constraints gives access but does not require any matching context."],3],["x34",0,2,"An ECS task on Fargate must read an S3 bucket from application code. The task definition has a task execution role that can pull images from ECR, but the app gets AccessDenied. What should be fixed?",["Add S3 read access to the ECR repository's resource policy","Attach an EC2 instance profile to the Fargate task definition","Apply a public-read bucket policy to the bucket for the task","Add a task role with S3 permissions for the application to use"],3,"The task execution role is used by the ECS agent to pull images and send logs; the task role supplies credentials to the application code. Adding S3 access to the execution role is the tempting mistake, as the app does not use those credentials.","Domain 2: ECS task roles",["The ECR repository policy governs image access and has nothing to do with S3 permissions.","Fargate tasks have no EC2 instances to attach a profile to; tasks use task roles.","Making the bucket public exposes data to everyone instead of granting the task least privilege.",null],3],["x35",0,2,"A Lambda function logs every incoming event in full, including Authorization headers that carry bearer tokens. What is the best fix?",["Stop logging raw events; log only needed fields and redact secrets","Shorten the log group's retention period to a single day","Encrypt the log group with a KMS key and keep logging the full event","Disable CloudWatch Logs for the function to hide the tokens"],0,"Sensitive values should never be written to logs; log only what troubleshooting needs and redact credentials. Encrypting the log group is tempting because it sounds secure, but authorized readers would still see tokens.","Domain 2: Keeping sensitive data out of logs",[null,"Short retention still exposes tokens to anyone reading logs during that window.","Encryption at rest protects storage, but anyone allowed to read the logs still sees the tokens.","Turning off logs removes the ability to troubleshoot rather than fixing what is being logged."],1],["x36",0,3,"In an AWS SAM template, which declaration tells CloudFormation to process SAM resource types such as AWS::Serverless::Function?",["AWSTemplateFormatVersion: '2010-09-09'","Resources: with a SAM Type property","Transform: AWS::Serverless-2016-10-31","Metadata: with a SAM tool section"],2,"The Transform line invokes the AWS::Serverless macro, which expands SAM resources into standard CloudFormation resources. AWSTemplateFormatVersion is tempting since it is also at the top of templates, but it does not expand anything.","Domain 3: AWS SAM template structure",["The format version identifies the template syntax but does not expand SAM resource types.","Serverless resources are declared under Resources, but they are expanded only because of the Transform.",null,"Metadata holds extra information about the template and does not trigger any processing."],1],["x37",0,3,"A developer ran sam deploy --guided once and wants later deployments to reuse the same stack name, Region and parameter values without prompts. Where does SAM store these choices?",["The Outputs section","buildspec.yml","samconfig.toml","appspec.yml"],2,"The guided deploy offers to save its answers to samconfig.toml, and later sam deploy runs read them. buildspec.yml is tempting because it is also a config file in the toolchain, but it belongs to CodeBuild.","Domain 3: SAM CLI configuration",["Outputs return values from a deployed stack; they do not hold deployment settings.","buildspec.yml configures CodeBuild builds, not SAM CLI deployments.",null,"appspec.yml configures CodeDeploy deployments and lifecycle hooks."],1],["x38",0,3,"The first cdk deploy into a new account and Region fails because resources the CDK needs, such as an S3 bucket for assets, do not exist. What should be run first?",["cdk bootstrap","cdk synth --all","cdk diff","cdk init"],0,"cdk bootstrap deploys the toolkit stack (staging bucket, roles and related resources) that CDK deployments need in each account and Region. cdk synth is tempting because it is also run before deploys, but it only writes local output.","Domain 3: AWS CDK bootstrap",[null,"synth only produces the template locally; it creates nothing in the account.","diff compares with a deployed stack and does not create the missing resources.","init creates a new local project skeleton, not account resources."],1],["x39",0,3,"Without using CodeDeploy, a developer wants the prod alias of a Lambda function to send 5% of invocations to version 8 and the rest to version 7. What should they configure?",["Two aliases that share the same name, one for each of the versions","A Lambda layer that points the function at the code of version 8","Reserved concurrency on version 8 equal to 5% of the account limit","Weighted routing on the alias, adding version 8 as an extra version"],3,"A Lambda alias can point to two versions with a routing configuration that sends a set weight to the additional version. CodeDeploy automates the same mechanism. Reserved concurrency is tempting but controls capacity, not traffic share.","Domain 3: Lambda weighted aliases",["Alias names must be unique within a function, so two aliases cannot share a name.","Layers hold shared code or dependencies; they do not route invocations between versions.","Concurrency settings cap scaling and do not split traffic by percentage.",null],2],["x40",0,3,"A team wants to send 10% of a REST API stage's traffic to a new API deployment and compare its metrics before promoting it. Which API Gateway feature fits?",["A mock integration on the API","A canary release on the stage","A usage plan for the stage","An edge-optimized endpoint type"],1,"Stage canary settings route a chosen percentage of requests to a canary deployment, with separate logs and metrics, and can then be promoted. Usage plans are tempting because they also attach to stages, but they only meter usage.","Domain 3: API Gateway canary releases",["Mock integrations return fixed responses without a backend; they do not split traffic.",null,"Usage plans meter and throttle API key holders; they do not route between deployments.","The endpoint type controls how clients reach the API, not how traffic splits across deployments."],1],["x41",0,3,"In a CodeDeploy blue/green deployment to Amazon ECS, the team wants tests to run against the replacement task set through the test listener before any production traffic is shifted. Which lifecycle hook should invoke the test function?",["AfterAllowTestTraffic","BeforeInstall","ApplicationStart","ValidateService"],0,"ECS appspec hooks are BeforeInstall, AfterInstall, AfterAllowTestTraffic, BeforeAllowTraffic and AfterAllowTraffic. AfterAllowTestTraffic runs once the test listener sends traffic to the new tasks. ValidateService is tempting from EC2 deployments but does not apply.","Domain 3: CodeDeploy hooks for ECS",[null,"BeforeInstall runs before the replacement task set is created, so there is nothing to test yet.","ApplicationStart is an EC2/on-premises hook and is not available for ECS deployments.","ValidateService is an EC2/on-premises hook; ECS deployments use the traffic-based hooks instead."],3],["x42",0,3,"A CodeDeploy deployment for a Lambda alias must run a validation function before any traffic shifts to the new version. Which appspec hook should call it?",["AfterInstall","ApplicationStop","BeforeAllowTraffic","DownloadBundle"],2,"Lambda deployments support two hooks: BeforeAllowTraffic (before shifting) and AfterAllowTraffic (after). AfterInstall is tempting from EC2 appspec files, but Lambda deployments do not have it.","Domain 3: CodeDeploy hooks for Lambda",["AfterInstall is not one of the hooks a Lambda deployment supports.","ApplicationStop is an EC2/on-premises hook for stopping the old application.",null,"DownloadBundle is a CodeDeploy-managed EC2 event and cannot run scripts or functions."],2],["x43",0,3,"An Elastic Beanstalk production app must keep full serving capacity during deployments, but the team wants lower cost than launching a complete second set of instances. Which deployment policy fits best?",["Immutable","Rolling with an additional batch","All at once","Rolling"],1,"Rolling with an additional batch first launches one extra batch, so capacity never drops, while only adding one batch of instances. Immutable is the tempting answer because it also preserves capacity, but it doubles the fleet during deployment.","Domain 3: Elastic Beanstalk deployment policies",["Immutable keeps capacity but launches a full parallel set of instances, the extra cost the team wants to avoid.",null,"All at once takes every instance out of service together, so capacity drops to zero briefly.","Plain rolling takes batches out of service, so capacity is reduced during the deployment."],3],["x44",0,3,"A team wants Elastic Beanstalk to send a percentage of client traffic to instances running the new version for an evaluation period, and to go back if the new instances are unhealthy. Which policy fits?",["Rolling","All at once","Rolling with an additional batch","Traffic splitting"],3,"Traffic-splitting deployments launch new instances and send a set percentage of traffic to them for an evaluation time; if they fail health checks, traffic returns to the old version. Rolling with an additional batch is tempting but has no canary-style split.","Domain 3: Elastic Beanstalk traffic splitting",["Rolling replaces instances batch by batch without sending a controlled share of traffic to the new version.","All at once switches every instance at once with no evaluation period.","This keeps capacity during a rolling update but does not split traffic for evaluation.",null],2],["x45",0,3,"A CodeBuild project compiles a Java application, but the next pipeline stage receives an empty output artifact. The buildspec has install and build phases only. What is missing?",["A post_build phase that echoes a message","An env section that defines the JAVA_HOME variable","A cache section for Maven dependencies","An artifacts section listing the output files"],3,"CodeBuild only uploads files named in the buildspec artifacts section (for example target/*.jar). Without it the output artifact is empty. A post_build phase is tempting since it runs last, but it does not package output.","Domain 3: CodeBuild buildspec artifacts",["A post_build phase runs commands but does not by itself declare which files become the artifact.","The build ran, so the environment is fine; env variables do not publish outputs.","Caching speeds up builds but has nothing to do with which files are handed to the next stage.",null],2],["x46",0,3,"A buildspec needs a database password during integration tests, and the password must not be stored in the repository. What is the best approach?",["Write the password directly into the buildspec.yml file","Reference it in the buildspec env section from Secrets Manager","Commit it to the repository inside an encrypted zip file","Enter it as a plaintext environment variable in the console"],1,"The buildspec env section can pull values from Secrets Manager (secrets-manager) or Parameter Store (parameter-store) at build time, so nothing sensitive is committed. A plaintext console variable is tempting but exposes the value.","Domain 3: CodeBuild secrets handling",["Anything written into buildspec.yml ends up in source control, which the requirement forbids.",null,"An encrypted zip still stores the secret in the repository and needs its own key handling.","Plaintext project variables are visible to anyone who can view the build project configuration."],2],["x47",0,3,"A CloudFormation stack update fails partway through. What does CloudFormation do by default?",["Keeps the updated resources and deletes the stack","Retries the failed resource until the update succeeds","Rolls the stack back to its last known working state","Deletes the stack along with all of its resources"],2,"By default a failed stack update is rolled back, returning resources to their last stable configuration (UPDATE_ROLLBACK_COMPLETE). Deleting the stack is a tempting guess because failed creates can end in deletion, but updates roll back.","Domain 3: CloudFormation rollback",["CloudFormation does not keep a half-updated stack and delete it; it restores the previous state.","There is no endless retry; the failed update triggers a rollback.",null,"Deleting the whole stack on a failed update would destroy working resources; that is not the default."],1],["x48",0,3,"A team published Lambda version 5 and pointed the prod alias at it. A developer then edits the code in the console without publishing. What do callers of the prod alias run?",["Version 5, since published versions cannot change","The edited code, because aliases follow $LATEST","An error until a new version has been published","Versions 5 and $LATEST alternately, request by request"],0,"Published versions are immutable snapshots, and an alias keeps pointing at the version it names. Edits affect only $LATEST until a new version is published and the alias updated. Assuming aliases track $LATEST is the common trap.","Domain 3: Lambda versions and aliases",[null,"An alias points to the version it was set to; only an unqualified call or $LATEST uses the edited code.","Editing $LATEST does not break an alias that points at a published version.","Traffic is split only when a routing configuration is set on the alias."],2],["x49",0,3,"A pipeline deploys a serverless API to a test stage. The team wants the pipeline to stop before production if end-to-end tests against that deployed stage fail. What should they add?",["A manual approval action placed before the test stage deployment","A CodeBuild test action after the test deploy that fails on test errors","Extra unit tests in the build phase that use mocked AWS services","CloudFormation drift detection run against the test stack afterwards"],1,"A test action (for example, CodeBuild running the integration suite against the test stage URL) that fails the stage on any failing test stops the pipeline before production. Mocked unit tests are tempting but do not exercise the deployed environment.","Domain 3: Integration testing in pipelines",["A manual approval before deployment tests nothing and relies on a person rather than results.",null,"Unit tests with mocks run before deployment and cannot prove the deployed stage works end to end.","Drift detection finds configuration changed outside CloudFormation; it does not test API behavior."],2],["x50",0,4,"A high-traffic API instrumented with X-Ray produces a large volume of traces and rising cost. The team wants a small representative sample, but every request on one critical checkout path. What should they configure?",["Disable active tracing on every function behind the API","Record all details as metadata instead of as annotations","Increase the buffer size used by the X-Ray daemon","Custom sampling rules: a low default rate plus a higher-priority checkout rule"],3,"X-Ray sampling rules match on attributes such as URL path and are evaluated by priority, so one rule can sample checkout at a high rate while the default rule samples everything else sparingly. Disabling tracing cuts cost but loses the needed data.","Domain 4: X-Ray sampling rules",["Turning tracing off loses visibility entirely, including for the critical path.","Metadata versus annotations changes indexing, not how many traces are recorded.","Buffer size affects how the daemon batches uploads, not how many requests are sampled.",null],3],["x51",0,4,"A developer wants X-Ray to show how long a function spends calling a third-party payment API compared with its own processing. What should they add?",["A subsegment around the payment API call","An annotation that stores the duration","A metric filter on the function's log group","A second X-Ray group for payment traces"],0,"Subsegments record timing for a specific downstream call or block of code within a segment, so the trace timeline shows that call separately. An annotation is tempting but only indexes values for filtering.","Domain 4: X-Ray subsegments",[null,"An annotation adds a searchable value but does not produce a timed block in the trace timeline.","A metric filter counts log patterns and does not appear in the trace breakdown.","Groups filter traces by expression; they do not time a section of code."],2],["x52",0,4,"An application needs a custom CloudWatch metric with 1-second granularity and an alarm that evaluates every 10 seconds. What should the developer use?",["A standard-resolution metric that is published every second","A scheduled CloudWatch Logs Insights query","A high-resolution metric with a high-resolution alarm","The latency figures on the X-Ray service map"],2,"Publishing with StorageResolution 1 creates a high-resolution metric, and alarms on it can use 10- or 30-second periods. Publishing a standard metric every second is tempting, but CloudWatch aggregates it to one minute.","Domain 4: High-resolution custom metrics",["Standard-resolution metrics are stored at 1-minute granularity, however often they are published.","Logs Insights queries are for ad hoc analysis, not 10-second alarm evaluation.",null,"The service map visualizes traces; it does not provide custom metrics or alarms."],2],["x53",0,4,"A Lambda function writes to a legacy database that accepts only a limited number of connections. During traffic spikes the function scales out and overwhelms the database. What is the simplest control?",["Enable provisioned concurrency on the function alias","Increase the function's memory to speed each call","Set reserved concurrency to cap how many copies run","Add an X-Ray annotation to the database requests"],2,"Reserved concurrency sets a maximum number of concurrent executions for the function, protecting a fragile downstream resource. Provisioned concurrency is the tempting mix-up; it is about warm starts, not a ceiling.","Domain 4: Lambda reserved concurrency",["Provisioned concurrency pre-warms environments; it does not stop the function scaling beyond that number.","Faster calls do not limit how many run at once, so connections can still spike.",null,"Annotations help filter traces but do not control scaling."],2],["x54",0,4,"After a team sets a large reserved concurrency value on one Lambda function, other functions in the same account and Region start being throttled during peaks. Why?",["Reserved concurrency is taken out of the unreserved pool the other functions share","Reserved concurrency doubles the memory of every other function in the Region","Setting reserved concurrency turns off scaling for all other functions in the account","Reserved concurrency makes cold starts longer for every function in the account"],0,"Account concurrency is shared per Region. Reserving a chunk for one function removes it from the unreserved pool, so other functions can hit the lower remaining ceiling and throttle. Assuming scaling was turned off is tempting, but they are simply short of pool.","Domain 4: Lambda concurrency troubleshooting",[null,"Concurrency settings never change any function's memory.","Other functions still scale, but only within what is left of the unreserved pool.","Cold start time is unrelated; the throttling comes from reduced available concurrency."],3],["x55",0,4,"After fixing a bug, a developer wants to move messages from an SQS dead-letter queue back to the source queue for reprocessing with minimal code. What should they use?",["Purge the DLQ and ask the producers to resend every message","Convert the dead-letter queue into a FIFO queue","Extend the dead-letter queue's retention period","The dead-letter queue redrive to the source queue"],3,"SQS supports DLQ redrive, which moves messages from the dead-letter queue back to the source queue (or another queue) from the console or API. Extending retention is tempting as a safety step but reprocesses nothing.","Domain 4: SQS dead-letter queue redrive",["Purging deletes the failed messages, and producers may not be able to recreate them.","A queue's type cannot be changed after creation, and doing so would not move messages.","Longer retention keeps messages but does not move them back for processing.",null],1],["x56",0,4,"A team wants an alarm whenever the text 'PaymentDeclined' appears in application logs more than 20 times in 5 minutes. What should be created first?",["An X-Ray group with a filter expression for payment declined errors","A metric filter on the log group counting those events","A saved CloudWatch Logs Insights query for the text","A CloudTrail trail that captures application events"],1,"A metric filter turns matching log events into a CloudWatch metric, and an alarm can then fire when the count exceeds 20 in 5 minutes. A saved Logs Insights query is tempting but is an analysis tool, not a metric.","Domain 4: CloudWatch metric filters and alarms",["X-Ray groups filter traces, not arbitrary log text.",null,"Saved Insights queries are run on demand and do not produce a metric to alarm on by themselves.","CloudTrail records AWS API calls, not text written to application logs."],2],["x57",0,4,"A new application has unpredictable DynamoDB traffic that goes from near zero to sudden large spikes, and the team does not want to manage capacity. Which option fits?",["On-demand capacity mode","Provisioned mode, low fixed capacity","Provisioned mode, scheduled scaling","DAX without any DynamoDB table"],0,"On-demand mode charges per request and adapts to traffic automatically, suiting spiky, unpredictable workloads with no capacity planning. Scheduled scaling is tempting but only helps when peaks are predictable.","Domain 4: DynamoDB capacity modes",[null,"A low fixed provisioned value will throttle during the spikes.","Scheduled scaling needs predictable timing, which this workload lacks.","DAX is a cache that sits in front of a table; it cannot replace one."],1],["x58",0,4,"A Lambda function was attached to private subnets that have no NAT gateway and no VPC endpoints. Now its DynamoDB calls hang until the function logs 'Task timed out'. What is the best fix?",["Raise the timeout to 15 minutes so the calls have time to finish","Increase memory so the function gets more network bandwidth","Add a DynamoDB gateway VPC endpoint to the subnet route tables","Enable active X-Ray tracing on the function to fix connectivity"],2,"A VPC-attached function uses the VPC's routing; with no NAT or endpoint it cannot reach public service endpoints. A DynamoDB gateway endpoint adds a private route. Raising the timeout is tempting but the requests will never complete.","Domain 4: Troubleshooting Lambda timeouts in a VPC",["The calls can never reach DynamoDB without a route, so a longer timeout only delays the failure.","More memory does not create a network path out of the private subnets.",null,"Tracing can show where time goes but does not provide connectivity."],3],["x59",0,4,"A developer wants to find which Lambda invocations used the most memory in the last day. Where is this data most readily available?",["CloudTrail management events recorded for the function","The REPORT log lines, queried with Logs Insights","The X-Ray service map for the application","IAM Access Analyzer findings for the role"],1,"Every invocation writes a REPORT line with duration, billed duration and max memory used, and Logs Insights exposes fields such as @maxMemoryUsed for sorting. X-Ray is tempting for performance questions but does not report memory.","Domain 4: CloudWatch Logs Insights",["Management events record API calls such as UpdateFunctionConfiguration, not per-invocation memory use.",null,"The service map shows service connections and latency, not memory used per invocation.","Access Analyzer reviews permissions and external access, not runtime metrics."],2],["x60",0,4,"A Java Lambda function has long cold starts caused by heavy initialization. The team wants to reduce them without paying to keep environments warm all the time. Which feature fits?",["Provisioned concurrency on the function alias","Reserved concurrency on the function","A larger ephemeral /tmp storage size","Lambda SnapStart on published versions"],3,"SnapStart snapshots the initialized execution environment when a version is published and resumes from it, cutting cold start time without always-on environments. Provisioned concurrency is the tempting answer but it is the pay-to-stay-warm option ruled out.","Domain 4: Lambda cold starts",["Provisioned concurrency works but keeps environments initialized continuously, which the team wants to avoid paying for.","Reserved concurrency caps scaling and has no effect on initialization time.","More /tmp space does not speed up initialization of the runtime or code.",null],3]]);
